   "title": "The Voice of the Martyrs: Prayers of the Persecuted",
   "url": "https://www.thegospelcoalition.org/article/voice-martyrs-prayers-persecuted/",
   "category": "devotional",
   "source": "The Gospel Coalition",
   "source_url": "https://www.thegospelcoalition.org/feed/",
   "published": "2026-11-06",
   "summary": "We pray for justice, but we do so as forgiven sinners.",
   "summary_zh": "我们祈求正义，但我们是作为被饶恕的罪人祈求正义。",
   "title_zh": "殉道者的声音：受迫害者的祈祷",
   "content": "“How long, O LORD? . . . Pour out your anger. . . . Let the avenging of the outpoured blood of your servants be known among the nations before our eyes!” (Ps. 79:5–6, 10). As you’re reading through Psalms and you stumble across Psalm 79, you can’t help but wonder what to do with this very Old Testament-sounding prayer. Surely, Asaph needed to be educated in the gospel, right? Revelation 6:9–10 describes the martyrs in heaven who have been slain for their testimony to the gospel and places Psalm 79 on their lips: “O Sovereign Lord, holy and true, how long before you will judge and avenge our blood on those who dwell on the earth?” Scholars agree the prayer of Psalm 79 is the likely background for the prayer of the martyrs, furthering our appreciation of this as a prayer for the church. It’s a prayer of and for the persecuted church. As we stand alongside them, we have divine warrant to pray Psalm 79 on their behalf. Trevor Laurence explains , “The vengeance petition of Rev. 6:9–10 thus supplies a model for the ongoing prayer of Christ’s body on earth.” Rather than being contrary to New Testament ethics, then, Psalm 79 fuels the church’s longing for every wrong to be made right. Nevertheless, you may still wonder, How do I take up a psalm like this in prayer for my persecuted brothers and sisters without falling into vindictive rage and vengefulness? Thankfully, Psalm 79 provides the guidelines needed to answer this question. Pray in Response to Horrific Injustice Verses 1–4 catalog the horrors God’s people suffered at the hands of the Babylonians who destroyed their temple: They defiled God’s holy place, they gave the flesh of God’s faithful ones to the beasts of the earth, they poured out their blood like water and refused them a proper burial, and they were the cause of God’s people becoming a taunt to their neighbors. Psalm 79 is not prayed in response to petty grievances but birthed out of horrendous evil. Psalm 79 is not prayed in response to petty grievances but birthed out of horrendous evil. This has two implications. First, these prayers do not give license for us to pray such things against just anyone. Second, if we object to such prayers, they challenge us to ask whether we have ever faced anything close to the agonizing evil that gave birth to this prayer. As Jamie Grant posits , “Perhaps our reluctance to appropriate the psalms of enmity is rooted in the comfort of our lives.” If we knew the kind of horror Psalm 79 speaks of (and that our persecuted brothers and sisters endure daily), perhaps we wouldn’t be so quick to dismiss these psalms. Pray for the Glory of God As psalms like this respond to such evil, they are fundamentally concerned with God’s glory. We see this in Psalm 79:6, where the nations’ refusal to call on God’s name is the motive behind Asaph’s cry. Likewise, verse 9 cites the glory of God’s name and his “name’s sake” as a rationale for God to answer this request. And verse 10 is concerned that, at present, the onlooking nations say, “Where is their God?” So Asaph asks that God’s justice would be made known among the nations (v. 10), and that God’s people might praise him for it (v. 13). The glory of God is the ultimate end of the imprecatory psalms. Or as James Hamilton says , “The glory of God in salvation through judgment is the center of the theology of Psalms.” The same is true of imprecatory psalms like Psalm 79. Their controlling desire is “Hallowed be thy name.” Pray with a Humble Posture The imprecatory psalms are not prayed in a spirit of self-righteousness; those praying them understand their need for God’s grace. They pray for justice from a posture of humility, where they confess that this judgment is what they too deserve apart from God’s grace (vv. 8–9). This is not unlike the widow’s prayer for justice in Luke 18:1–8 being paired with the parable of the Pharisee and tax collector in verses 9–14. We pray for justice, but we do so as forgiven sinners. We pray the second petition of th",
   "content_zh": "\" 耶和华说,多久? ......让我们眼前的各国都了解你仆人流血的复仇!” 在阅读\"诗经\"和\"穿越\"\"诗经\"79\"时, 当然,阿萨夫需要接受福音教育,对吧?\n启示录 6:9-10描述了在天上因对福音的见证被活活打死的殉道者,并将\"诗经\"79放在了他们的嘴唇上:\"神圣而真实的君主啊,你还要多久才能判决和为居住在地上的人报仇? 学者们同意\"诗经·79\"的祈祷是烈士祈祷的可能背景,进一步让我们把这作为祈祷来欣赏.\n为教会。 这是受迫害的教会的祈祷。 当我们站在他们一边时,我们拥有代表他们祈祷的神圣授权。 Trevor Laurence解释道, “Rev.\n因此,6:9-10提供一种模式,用于在地球上不断祈祷基督的身体。 Psalm 79 并没有违背新约伦理,而是促使教会渴望一切错误都得到纠正。 尽管如此,你还是会想知道,我如何在为我受迫害的兄弟姐妹祈祷时接受这样的诗歌,而不陷入报复性的愤怒和复仇?\n值得庆幸的是,\"诗经\"79提供了回答这个问题所需的准则.\n愿上帝的百姓在巴比伦人手中遭受的恐怖, 他们玷污了真主的圣所,把真主的忠实的肉献给大地上的野兽,他们把自己的血洒出像水一样,并拒绝给他们安葬,他们是真主的宗族变成他们的邻居的嘲笑者的原因。\n诗篇79并不是针对小的不满而祈祷的,而是源于可怕的邪恶。 诗篇79不是针对小的怨愤而祈祷的,而是由可怕的邪恶所生. 这有两个影响。 首先,这些祷告不能让我们对任何人祈祷。\n第二,如果我们反对这种祈祷,他们要求我们问,我们是否曾遇到过任何与导致这种祈祷的令人痛苦的罪恶相近的事情。\n正如Jamie Grant所说 , “ 也许我们不愿接受敌意的诗句, 如果我们知道Psalm 79所说的恐怖(以及我们受迫害的兄弟姐妹每天忍受的恐怖),\n祈求上帝的荣耀,像这样的诗歌回应这种邪恶,他们从根本上关心上帝的荣耀. 我们在\"诗经\"79:6中看到这一点,在\"诗经\"中,各国拒绝呼唤上帝的名号是阿萨夫喊叫背后的动机. 同样地,第9节引用了上帝之名的荣耀及其 \" 为名 \" ,作为上帝回应这一要求的理由。\n或如詹姆斯·汉密尔顿所言,“通过判断拯救上帝的荣耀是诗歌神学的中心。” 般若波罗蜜经卷第七十九. 他们的欲望是“尊贵的名号”。 以低调的姿势祈祷 神圣的诗歌不是本着自以为是的精神祈祷的;祈祷者理解他们对上帝恩惠的需要.\n他们以谦卑姿态祈祷正义,他们承认,除了上帝的恩惠外,这种判决也是他们应得的(vv.8-9)。 这与Luke 18:1–8中的遗孀祈求公道, 我们祈祷正义,但我们这样做是宽恕罪人。 我们祈祷第二次请愿",
   "image": "https://media.thegospelcoalition.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/21201949/voice-martyrs-prayers-persecuted.jpg",
   "author": "Ryan Swale",
   "tags": [
    "Bible & Theology",
    "The Carson Center",
    "Old Testament",
