   "title": "Best Practices from Evangelistically Effective Churches",
   "url": "https://www.thegospelcoalition.org/article/best-practices-evangelism/",
   "category": "devotional",
   "source": "The Gospel Coalition",
   "source_url": "https://www.thegospelcoalition.org/feed/",
   "published": "2026-11-03",
   "summary": "While thousands of Americans are dechurching, some churches are baptizing many new members. Here’s how the country’s most evangelistically effective churches are sharing the gospel.",
   "summary_zh": "虽然成千上万的美国人正在脱离教会，但一些教会正在为许多新成员施洗。以下是这个国家最有效传福音的教会分享福音的方式。",
   "title_zh": "传福音有效教会的最佳实践",
   "content": "https://media.thegospelcoalition.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/23102213/effective-evangelism.mp3 In the summer of 2021, pastor Luke Peterson moved to Columbus, Ohio, to look for lost people. To find them, he joined a gym. He started building friendships. And then he began asking those friends if they wanted to come over. “Word started getting around—‘You’re not going to believe this, but Luke’s a pastor,’” he said. ‘It created some intrigue, and people started coming. I used to have a note in my phone of how many people came to our church because I went to CrossFit Grandview. It was up to like 60 or 70 before I stopped counting because it was impossible.” Peterson’s good at evangelism, and his church is too—last year, they welcomed around 500 first-time visitors. When he preaches, he knows about half his audience is probably unchurched. Pastor Luke Peterson (right) chats with Capital Church attendees / Courtesy of Capital Church Facebook This is unusual in a country where 40 million adults have stopped attending church over the last 30 years, and where 76 percent of pastors said “fostering connections with unchurched people” is a need in their church. Much has changed since the Billy Graham rallies of the ’60s, the door-knocking of the ’80s, and seeker-sensitive church billboards of the ’90s. In the past, “you could randomly walk up to the person pumping gas next to you and say, ‘Do you know Jesus?’” said Perimeter Church pastor Jeff Norris. “Could that still work? Sure. But more and more, we tell people to invite neighbors into their home. The reality is that evangelism is much slower, and more relational, than it used to be.” Today, people aren’t as likely to read the Bible, think it’s the Word of God, or go to church . But they’re also less hostile to Christianity than they were five years ago. And while they read the Bible less—especially in Gen Z—the trajectory is moving up . “We’re not past ‘my truth, your truth,’” said Rob Warren, pastor of Doxa Church in Madison, Wisconsin. “But people aren’t as sure that their truth is actually true.” These days, evangelistically effective churches are keeping evangelism front of mind in the way they preach, train, and encourage their congregations. They’re celebrating small wins. And they’re leading with a high view of Scripture, prayer, and God’s sovereignty. “We’ve seen all of our ministries grow—from kids to youth to college ministry to Doxa Church as a whole,” Warren said. “It’s not just one demographic of people who are getting saved. God’s doing something.” Times Have Changed Thirty years ago, evangelical churches leaned on programs like Evangelism Explosion or Continuous Witness Training to deliver evangelistic focus and training, church leader Thom Rainer said . Those structured initiatives fit well in a culture that was more Christian and more institutionalized—it took an average of 20 church members to reach one person for Christ per year. Today, it takes 50, Rainer said . “Evangelism efficacy, evangelism effectiveness, has definitely diminished.” That trend is likely to continue. As social media and AI fill our time and increase our social anxiety, even relational evangelism is growing more difficult. As social media and AI fill our time and increase our social anxiety, even relational evangelism is growing more difficult. “I’ve got a new couple that moved into my neighborhood a while ago, and I haven’t even seen them yet,” said Daniel Simmons, executive pastor of campuses and central discipleship at The Summit Church. This means equipping the congregation is more challenging—and important—than ever before. “Evangelism can be this thing that is so burdensome and guilt-driven,” said pastor Christian Gracia. He planted King’s City Church in Las Vegas in 2024 with around 80 people and has seen the church grow to more than 400. “People can feel like they’re not ready or not good enough at evangelism,” Gracia said. “They don’t always know what to say, and can feel like a terri",
   "content_zh": "https://media.thegospelcoalition.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/2310213/ 有效-福音主义.mp3 互联网档案馆的存檔,存档日期2013-12-21. 2021年夏天,牧师卢克·彼得森搬到了俄亥俄州哥伦布寻找迷路的人. 为了找到他们 他加入了健身房 他开始建立友谊. 然后他开始问那些朋友是否想过来 “Word开始四处走动, 它创造了一些阴谋,人们就来了。 我曾经有一张笔记在我的手机上 多少人来到我们的教堂 因为我去了克罗斯菲特大观。 在我停止计数之前, 彼得森擅长福音传道, 他的教堂去年也太高了, 当他传道时,他知道大约一半的观众可能毫无伤感. Luke Peterson牧师(右)与首都教会与会者/首都教会礼节的聊天 在过去30年里,有4000万成年人停止参加教会活动,76%的牧师说,“与无伤感的人建立关系”是他们教会的一项需要。 自20世纪60年代的比利·格雷厄姆集会、80年代的门锁和90年代对寻求者敏感的教堂告示牌以来,情况发生了很大变化。 过去,“你可以随机地走到你旁边抽气的人面前,说`你认识耶稣吗?' ” “这还能起作用吗? 当然 但越来越多的,我们告诉人们邀请邻居进入他们的家. 现实是,福音派比以前慢得多,关系更密切。” 今天,人们不太可能读到圣经, 他们对基督教的敌意也低于五年前。 当他们读少了圣经, 威斯康辛州麦迪逊市多克萨教堂的牧师Rob Warren说, “但人们并不那么确定他们的真实性。” 这些天,福音会有效的教会在他们的传道,训练,鼓励他们的会众的方式中,保持福音会的心智前沿. 他们正在庆祝小赢家。 他们以对圣经、祷告和上帝主权的高度评价为首。 “我们目睹了我们所有各部的成长, \" 不仅人口众多, 上帝正在做一些事情。 教会领袖Thom Rainer表示, 这些结构化的倡议非常适合一种更加基督教化和更加制度化的文化——平均每年需要20名教会成员为基督而接触一个人。 今天,它需要50,雷纳说。 “福音会的功效,福音会的功效,肯定已经下降。” 这一趋势很可能继续下去。 由于社交媒体和AI填补了我们的时间并增加了我们的社会焦虑,甚至连关系福音派也越来越困难. 由于社交媒体和AI填补了我们的时间并增加了我们的社会焦虑,甚至连关系福音派也越来越困难. “我有一对新夫妇不久前搬入我的社区, 我甚至还没有见到他们,” 这意味着为教会提供设备比以往任何时候更具挑战性和重要性。 基督教Gracia牧师说, 他于2024年在拉斯维加斯种植了国王城教堂,约有80人,并目睹了教堂发展到400多所. Gracia说, “人们会觉得自己还没有准备好, “他们并不总是知道该说什么,",
