  {
   "title": "Apologetics for Hipsters & Other Secularists",
   "url": "https://www.monergism.com/blog/apologetics-hipsters-other-secularists",
   "category": "giant",
   "source": "Monergism",
   "source_url": "https://www.monergism.com/blog/feed",
   "published": "2026-11-11",
   "summary": "\"Although They Knew God ... They Suppressed the Truth...\" The opportunities are wide open for Christians to speak with secular people these days, especially if you live in a metropolitan area. The average hipster you may meet in the medium to big sized city is a secularist who may listen to NPR, has a liberal, ecological, and/or anti-capitalist political ideology ... wears vintage clothing from a thrift store and consumes ethically. He or she often either rides a bike, uses public transportation",
   "summary_zh": "“虽然他们认识上帝……他们压制了真理……”如今，基督徒与世俗人交谈的机会非常广泛，特别是如果你住在大都市地区。在中型到大型城市中，您可能会遇到的平均时髦人士是世俗主义者，他们可能会听NPR ，拥有自由主义、生态主义和/或反资本主义的政治意识形态……穿着旧货店的复古服装，并以合乎道德的方式消费。他或她经常骑自行车，使用公共交通工具",
   "title_zh": "时髦人士和其他世俗主义者的护教学",
   "content": "\"Although They Knew God ... They Suppressed the Truth...\" The opportunities are wide open for Christians to speak with secular people these days, especially if you live in a metropolitan area. The average hipster you may meet in the medium to big sized city is a secularist who may listen to NPR, has a liberal, ecological, and/or anti-capitalist political ideology ... wears vintage clothing from a thrift store and consumes ethically. He or she often either rides a bike, uses public transportation or even may be driving a hybrid or bio-diesel vehicle. This person is deeply concerned with the ethical treatment of animals, is strongly against slavery, torture, racism and political oppression against women. In other words, he takes morality very seriously. Another characteristic of this individual is that he/she thinks Christianity is irrelevant at best. Since we deeply care about the eternal destinies of such individuals how should we go about reaching such a person with the gospel? Where would you start? Once of the more effective ways, I have found, is to remember that that your secular friend already knows God exists. He/she knows God exists and lives as if He does. In Romans chapter 1 Paul clearly teaches this about all people when he says, \"For although they knew God\" (verse 21) \"...by their unrighteousness suppress the truth\" (verse 18). But how can we demonstrate to your friends that they already believe in God, when intellectually they deny His existence? While there are many ways this could be done, I would like to suggest one way that I have found to be quite persuasive: The knowledge of God through their own morality.",
   "content_zh": "\"虽然他们认识上帝... 这些天基督徒与世俗人士交谈的机会是广阔的,特别是如果你生活在一个都市地区. 在中等至大城市中,你可能遇到的平凡的嬉皮士是一个世俗主义者,他可能听从NPR,具有自由主义,生态主义和/或反资本主义的政治意识形态.穿着节俭店出产的古装并合乎道德地消费. 他或她往往要么骑自行车,使用公共交通,要么甚至可能驾驶混合或生物柴油车辆. 此人对动物的道德待遇深感关切,强烈反对奴役、酷刑、种族主义和对妇女的政治压迫。 换句话说,他非常重视道德。 这个人的另一个特征是,heshe认为基督教最多是无关紧要的. 既然我们深深地关心这些人的永恒命运,那么我们如何用福音去帮助这样一个人呢? 你从哪里开始? 我发现,最有效的方法之一 就是记住,你的世俗朋友已经知道上帝的存在。 他/她知道上帝的存在 并活得像他一样 在罗马人第一章中,保罗明确教导了这一点,他说:\"虽然他们认识上帝\"(第21节)\"......以不义压制真理\"(第18节). 但我们怎能向你的朋友证明,他们已经信仰了真主,而他们在理智上否认他的存在呢? 虽然有许多方法可以做到这一点,但我想建议一种方法,我认为它很有说服力: 以自德而知神.",
   "image": "https://www.monergism.com/thethreshold/sdg/eBook_Library_HP.jpg",
   "author": "john_hendryx",
   "tags": [],
   "language": "en",
   "country": "US",
   "region": "",
   "stance": "evangelical",
   "flags": [],
   "priority": 2,
   "fetched_at": "2026-11-11T00:26:01.362437",
   "extra": {
    "feed_type": "rss20"
   },
   "id": "1eb50cbeb7a6f7d7"
  },
  {
   "title": "Praying for Answers",
   "url": "https://odb.org/2026/11/03/",
   "category": "devotional",
   "source": "Our Daily Bread (English)",
   "source_url": "https://odb.org/feed/",
   "published": "2026-11-11",
   "summary": "When Monica’s son Augustine wanted to leave home for a city known for its immorality, she pleaded with God not to let him go. But he went anyway, which greatly grieved his mother, yet it was there that God transformed his life. In his Confessions, Augustine later prayed, “Yet in your deep counsel you heard the central point of her longing . . . [so] that you would make me what she continually prayed for.”",
   "summary_zh": "当莫妮卡的儿子奥古斯丁想离开家到一个以不道德闻名的城市时，她恳求上帝不要让他离开。但无论如何，他还是去了，这让他的母亲非常伤心，但正是在那里，神改变了他的生活。奥古斯丁后来在他的《忏悔录》中祈祷说： “然而，在你深刻的劝告中，你听到了她渴望的中心点……这样你就能使我成为她不断祈祷的东西。”",
   "title_zh": "祈求答案",
   "content": "When Monica’s son Augustine wanted to leave home for a city known for its immorality, she pleaded with God not to let him go. But he went anyway, which greatly grieved his mother, yet it was there that God transformed his life. In his Confessions, Augustine later prayed, “Yet in your deep counsel you heard the central point of her longing . . . [so] that you would make me what she continually prayed for.”",
   "content_zh": "当莫妮卡的儿子奥古斯丁想离开家到一个以不道德闻名的城市时，她恳求上帝不要让他离开。但无论如何，他还是去了，这让他的母亲非常伤心，但正是在那里，神改变了他的生活。奥古斯丁后来在他的《忏悔录》中祈祷说： “然而，在你深刻的劝告中，你听到了她渴望的中心点……这样你就能使我成为她不断祈祷的东西。”",
   "image": "https://odb.org/globalassets/global/logos/parent-logos/odb_parentbrand_logo_8-9-24_fullclr_icon.png?width=1200&height=680&rmode=max",
   "author": "James Banks",
