  {
   "title": "Discerning Between Godly and Worldly Grief",
   "url": "https://www.9marks.org/article/discerning-between-godly-and-worldly-grief/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=discerning-between-godly-and-worldly-grief",
   "category": "devotional",
   "source": "9Marks",
   "source_url": "https://9marks.org/feed/",
   "published": "2026-11-11",
   "summary": "Godly grief leads to mourning over sin, not simply its consequences.   The post  Discerning Between Godly and Worldly Grief  appeared first on  9Marks .",
   "summary_zh": "敬虔的悲伤会导致对罪的哀悼，而不仅仅是对罪的后果的哀悼。文章《辨别虔诚与世俗的悲伤》首次出现在九分榜上。",
   "title_zh": "分辨虔诚与世俗的悲伤",
   "content": "Abstract: Sam Crites offers three ways to help pastors distinguish between worldly grief and godly grief based on Paul’s words in 2 Corinthians 7:10. Only godly grief produces true repentance, and such grief can be discerned by mourning over sin, not merely its consequences; a genuine desire for reconciliation, no matter the cost; and an earnest desire to please God, not the world. The fruit of godly grief usually becomes evident over time through ongoing repentance and faith in Christ. Editor’s Note: This article is taken from Church Matters, the 9Marks journal for pastors, in the issue titled Conversion . I was discipled by a man named Steve Tucker. He was a simple man full of simple lessons, and one of those simple lessons has stuck with me: a true mark of genuine conversion and its effects is how willing someone is to repent of their sin. However, genuine repentance is a tricky thing. When I catch my children doing something wrong, they are more than willing to “repent,” especially when punishment is imminent. They go from enjoying their wrongdoing to shedding tears. But it’s not always easy to tell if they are grieved over upsetting me or because they know there will be consequences for their actions. Pastors have to make a similar determination when a member is confronted for their sin. Is this person shedding tears only because they were caught in sin, or are they truly repenting? Both kinds of grief can look the same, at least on the surface, but only one leads to life. Those who refuse to repent give evidence that they haven’t been converted. So how can we tell the difference? Three Marks of Godly Grief Since we cannot see the heart of a man, we must look at the fruit of his life—the marks of repentance that accompany genuine conversion. Heart change, not behavior modification, is always the aim. Therefore, we must discern the external evidences of the Spirit’s internal work. This is where Paul’s words to the Corinthians can help us out. He says, “For godly grief produces a repentance that leads to salvation without regret, whereas worldly grief produces death” (2 Cor. 7:10). Notice that genuine repentance is produced by “godly grief.” Allow me, then, to put forward three marks of repentance from 2 Corinthians 7 that are evidence of godly grief. This list is not meant to be exhaustive but merely a helpful tool for discerning true repentance and, therefore, genuine conversion. 1. Godly Grief Leads to Mourning over Sin, Not Simply Its Consequences In 2 Corinthians 7, Paul describes how restless and anxious he was as he awaited the Corinthians’ response to his previous letter. But when Titus came, Paul was comforted because his report brought news of the grief of the Corinthians—not grief over the harshness of Paul’s letter but grief over their own sin. We’re reminded that godly grief is first and foremost a response to sin and not merely its consequences. David says to God in Psalm 51:4, “Against you, you only, have I sinned and done what is evil in your sight.” How could David be so insensitive? What about Uriah and Bathsheba? Or the child who died? David was aware of how the consequences of his sin affected others, but he felt greater anguish in his heart over how he had offended the Lord. And this should be true for all who repent: they should care most about how their sin has offended their Lord. This is the fruit of understanding the gospel, as the sufferings of Christ on the cross were the payment due for our sin. On the other hand, a preoccupation with the sin of others, a tendency to shift the blame to someone else, or grief that is only short-lived—all these are indications that an individual is not mourning because they have offended the Lord, but instead because of the consequences of their sin. 2. Godly Grief Leads to a Genuine Desire for Reconciliation, No Matter the Cost The Corinthians were grieved that their sin had ruptured their relationship with Paul. Paul says that Titus reported “your longing, your ",
   "content_zh": "摘要:Sam Crites提供三种方法帮助牧师根据保罗在2 Corinthians 7:10中的话来区分世界性的悲伤和神性的悲伤. 只有上帝的悲痛才产生真正的忏悔,这种悲痛可以通过对罪恶的哀悼而分辨出来,而不仅仅是其后果;真正渴望和解,无论付出什么代价;真正渴望取悦上帝而不是世界。\n\n神所悲伤的果实通常通过不断的忏悔和对基督的信仰而逐渐显现出. 编辑说明:这篇文章取自9Marks牧师杂志《教会事务》, 我被一个叫斯蒂夫·塔克的人徒弟\n\n他是一个充满了简单教训的简单人,而其中的一门简单教训一直粘在我身上:真正皈依的真正标志,其影响在于一个人多么愿意悔改自己的罪恶. 然而,真正的忏悔是一个棘手的问题。 当我发现我的孩子们做错事时,他们更愿意“悔改”,特别是在惩罚迫在眉睫的时候。\n\n他们从享受错失行为到流泪 或因为他们知道自己的行动将带来后果。 当一个成员面对他们的罪恶时,牧师必须作出类似的决定。 这个人眼泪是因为他们陷入了罪恶之中,还是他们真正悔改了?\n\n这两种悲伤可以看起来一样,至少在表面,但只有一种会导致生命. 拒绝悔改的人, 那么,我们如何辨别区别呢? 由于我们看不到一个人的心,我们必须看看他生命的果实——真正的皈依伴随着悔改的痕迹。\n\n心事改变,不是行为改变,总是目标. 因此,我们必须找出精神内部工作的外部证据。 保罗对科林斯人的话, 他说,“由于上帝的悲痛导致悔改,导致无悔地得到救赎,而世界的悲痛则导致死亡”(第7:10节)。\n\n注意真正的忏悔是“神圣的悲痛”。 那么,请允许我提出2个哥林多7号的忏悔标记,这是上帝悲痛的证据。 这份清单并非详尽无遗,而只是了解真正悔改,从而了解真正皈依的有用工具。 1. 联合国\n\n保罗描述他在等待科林斯人对他的前一封信的答复时, 但当提图斯来时,保罗感到欣慰,因为他的报告带来了科林斯人悲痛的消息——不是对保罗信的严酷性感到悲痛,而是对自己的罪行感到悲痛.\n\n大卫知道自己的罪孽的后果如何影响他人,但是他因为自己是如何冒犯了主而感到了更大的痛苦. 这对于悔罪的人们是真实的,他们应当最关心他们的罪过是如何侵犯他们的主的。 这是理解福音的结晶,因为基督在十字架上所受之苦是对我们的罪所应得的报酬.\n\n另一方面,关注他人的罪过,把责任转移给别人的倾向,或只是短暂的悲伤——所有这些都表明个人不是因为冒犯了主而哀悼,而是因为他们的罪过的后果. 2. 联合国\n\n上帝的悲伤导致真正的和解愿望, 无关紧要的代价 科林斯人感到悲伤 他们的罪恶破坏了他们与保罗的关系。 保罗说,Titus报告说,“你的渴望,你的...",
   "image": "https://www.9marks.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Discerning-Between-Godly-and-Worldly-Grief--scaled.jpg",
   "author": "Jeri Choi",
   "tags": [],
   "language": "en",
   "country": "US",
   "region": "",
   "stance": "evangelical",
   "flags": [],
   "priority": 2,
   "fetched_at": "2026-11-11T00:25:58.742967",
   "extra": {
    "feed_type": "rss20"
   },
   "id": "03bbdf77472c6923"
  },
  {
   "title": "Why the Doctrine of Conversion Matters for Membership Interviews",
   "url": "https://www.9marks.org/article/why-the-doctrine-of-conversion-matters-for-membership-interviews/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=why-the-doctrine-of-conversion-matters-for-membership-interviews",
   "category": "devotional",
   "source": "9Marks",
   "source_url": "https://9marks.org/feed/",
   "published": "2026-11-11",
   "summary": "It’s difficult to discern whether someone has been converted if you’re hazy on what conversion is.   The post  Why the Doctrine of Conversion Matters for Membership Interviews  appeared first on  9Marks .",
   "summary_zh": "如果你不知道什么是皈依，就很难辨别某人是否已经皈依。帖子Why the Doctrine of Conversion Matters for Membership Interviews首次出现在9Marks上。",
   "title_zh": "为什么转换原则对会员面试很重要",
   "content": "Abstract: David Burnette explains why the doctrine of conversion is so crucial for pastors and churches as they carry out their responsibilities in affirming new members as believers. We must not only understand what conversion is but also be able to apply it to the lives of real people. After giving some basic reminders about membership interviews and identifying things that we are not looking for as evidence of conversion, Burnette provides four questions to help us evaluate whether a prospective member has been converted. Editor’s Note: This article is taken from Church Matters, the 9Marks journal for pastors, in the issue titled Conversion . “I wasn’t living right. I was getting drunk, partying, just doing whatever I wanted. And then it was like God just stopped me in my tracks. I knew something had to change. That’s when I started coming to church, and one Sunday, I felt like the preacher was talking directly to me. Right then I knew I needed to become a Christian, and I got baptized a few weeks later. That was about a year ago, and ever since then I’ve pretty much been coming to church and trying to do what’s right.” That’s how Ben responded when you asked him how he became a Christian. Ben recently moved to your city, and he just completed your church’s four-week New Members Class. Now, as the final step in the process—often called a “membership interview”—you as a pastor must decide whether you will recommend Ben for membership at the next members’ meeting. For the purposes of this article, I’m concerned primarily with the question of whether Ben is converted. I’m assuming he meets your church’s other membership requirements. No doubt you’ve heard similar testimonies, even if the details differ. There’s been some kind of change in the person’s life, and they seem sincere, but it’s not yet clear that this change was rooted in the gospel. So you’ll need to do some more probing. Putting Our Doctrine of Conversion to Work Testimonies like Ben’s remind us why having a biblical doctrine of conversion is so crucial when it comes to the church’s responsibility to affirm new members as believers. After all, it’s difficult to discern whether someone has been converted if you’re hazy on what conversion is . For an explanation of the doctrine of conversion, see Michael Lawrence, Conversion (Crossway, 2017); see also the article by Gregg Allison titled “You Can’t Make I t Happen: The Spirit and the Doctrine of Conversion” in this issue of Church Matters . Testimonies like Ben’s also remind us that we must be able to apply our doctrine of conversion to real-life people in real-life situations. Some prospective members will be new Christians, while others will be less mature in their faith; some will have been converted for years but still have significant blind spots; still others will think they’re Christians when they haven’t truly been converted. And those aren’t the only complicating factors. Some will have a hard time articulating the very truths they profess; others will fail to mention basic aspects of the gospel in their interview; more than a few will be unsure as to how and when they recognized their need for a Savior. All these factors (and more) mean that wisdom and discernment are needed when we are interviewing prospective members. Reminders for Our Approach Before thinking through some practical ways to probe further with testimonies like Ben’s, a few basic reminders may be helpful. Your Knowledge Is Limited Unlike Jesus, we can’t look into a person’s heart, which means our decisions about someone’s spiritual state are never infallible. The very fact that Jesus gave instructions about excommunication indicates that he knew the church would sometimes welcome in those who weren’t truly converted (see Matt. 18:15–18). Even the apostle Paul was wrong about his one-time co-worker Demas. Prior to Demas’s departure, Paul mentions him twice when he refers to his fellow workers in the gospel ministry (Col. 4:14; Ph lm. 24). Ou",
   "content_zh": "摘要:David Burnette解释为什么皈依学说对牧师和教会如此重要,因为他们履行自己的职责,确认新成员为信徒。 我们不仅必须了解什么是皈依,而且还必须能够将其应用于真实人民的生活。 在对成员面谈作了一些基本的提醒,并查明了我们没有寻找的东西作为皈依的证据之后,伯内特提出了四个问题来帮助我们评估未来的成员是否已经皈依。 编辑说明:这篇文章取自9Marks牧师杂志《教会事务》, “我生活不对。 我喝醉了 狂欢 随心所欲 然后它就像上帝 刚刚阻止我在我的轨道。 我知道有些事情必须改变 这时我开始去教堂, 然后我知道我要成为基督徒 几周后我接受了洗礼 那是一年前的事了, 从那时起, 我几乎一直在教堂里努力做正确的事。 问他如何成为基督徒。 班最近搬到了你的城市 他刚刚完成了你教会的四周新会员课 现在,作为这一进程的最后一步——通常称为 \" 成员面试 \" ——你作为牧师必须决定是否推荐本在下一次成员会议上成为成员。 为了本条的目的,我主要关心本是否皈依的问题。 我想他符合你们教会的其他会员条件。 即使细节不同, 但还不清楚这种变化是否源于福音。 因此,你需要做一些更多的调查。 将本的《皈依论》付诸实践, 毕竟很难分辨是否有人皈依, 关于皈依学说的解释,见Michael Lawrence, Transference (Crossway, 2017);另见Gregg Allison在“教会事务”一文中题为“You Can't Make I t Happen: The Spirit and the Transference of Church Matters”的文章。 本的证言也提醒我们,我们必须能够将我们的皈依学说运用到现实生活中的人身上。 有些未来成员将成为新基督徒, 而另一些则在信仰上不太成熟; 有些已经皈依多年, 这些并非唯一的复杂因素。 有些人将很难阐明他们所宣称的真相;另一些人在访谈中将不提及福音的基本方面;少数人将不确定他们如何和何时认识到他们需要救世主。 所有这些因素(以及更多因素)都意味着,当我们与可能的成员面谈时,需要智慧和分辨。 在想出一些切实可行的方法来进一步探究本所提供证词之前, 与耶稣不同, 我们无法调查一个人的心, 这意味着我们决定一个人的精神状态, Jesus给出的关于excommunication的指示本身就表明他知道教会有时会欢迎那些没有真正皈依的人(见Matt. 18:15–18). 就连使徒保罗也对他的一次性同工德马斯的看法是错误的. 在Demas离开之前,Paul两次提到他在福音部的同僚(Col. 4:14;Ph. 24)。 阴",
   "image": "https://www.9marks.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Why-the-Doctrine-of-Conversion-Matters-for-Membership-Interviews-1-scaled.jpg",
   "author": "Jeri Choi",
