  {
   "title": "Prison Didn’t Mean Paul Was Wrong",
   "url": "https://radical.net/article/prison-didnt-mean-wrong/",
   "category": "missionary",
   "source": "Radical (David Platt)",
   "source_url": "https://radical.net/feed/",
   "published": "2026-11-11",
   "summary": "Steven Morales explains why following Jesus is worth every sacrifice, even when faithfulness leads to suffering and imprisonment.",
   "summary_zh": "史蒂文·莫拉莱斯（ Steven Morales ）解释了为什么即使忠诚导致痛苦和监禁，跟随耶稣也是值得的。",
   "title_zh": "监狱并不意味着保罗错了",
   "content": "“I therefore, a prisoner for the Lord, urge you to walk in a manner worthy of the calling to which you have been called.” (Eph. 4:1) Those are weighty words. And they’re weighty not because Paul is writing from a quiet study or a place of comfort, but because he is writing them from a prison cell. Paul isn’t sending this letter from a position of safety or success as the world defines it. He’s writing as a man in chains—arrested for preaching the gospel and planting churches. To his critics, he was a troublemaker. A dangerous agitator. To God, he was a faithful servant . That matters. Paul is suffering for the very message he’s urging the church in Ephesus to remember and live out. His words carry weight because his life backs them up. He’s not asking them to do anything he himself isn’t already doing—at great personal cost. And here’s the remarkable thing about Paul: there is no scenario in which he loses. Let him go free, and he’ll travel to the ends of the earth preaching Christ and starting churches. Lock him up, and he’ll become “a prisoner for the Lord,” turning his suffering into a living testimony of the worth of the gospel. Execute him? Fine. As he says elsewhere, “For to me to live is Christ, and to die is gain” (Phil. 1:21, ESV). There is no situation where Paul says, “The gospel isn’t enough anymore.” There is no moment where he concludes, “This just isn’t worth it.” Instead, Paul’s entire life—especially his suffering—testifies to this truth: no matter your circumstances, trusting in the gospel is never wasted. Paul wasn’t in prison because his ministry failed. He was in prison because he believed the message was worth suffering for. And that’s an important distinction. If a message isn’t worth suffering for, it’s probably not worth believing in. Christianity doesn’t promise a life without pain. What it promises is something better: a suffering that is not meaningless. A hope that suffering does not get the final word. That’s why Paul can say this in 2 Corinthians: “For this light momentary affliction is preparing for us an eternal weight of glory beyond all comparison, as we look not to the things that are seen but to the things that are unseen. For the things that are seen are transient, but the things that are unseen are eternal.” (2 Co. 4:17–18) The gospel gives us a framework for enduring temporary suffering because it anchors us in permanent promises. We can endure hardship now because we know it won’t last forever. Our pain has an expiration date . Why? Because Jesus has already suffered fully in our place. He took sin, death, and judgment upon himself—and walked out of the grave victorious. Because of him, not even death has the final say over us. So when Paul urges believers to “walk in a manner worthy of the calling,” he’s not laying a burden on their shoulders. He’s inviting them into a life shaped by hope—a life that knows that whatever it costs to follow Jesus, it will never be in vain. This article first appeared in The Commission , Radical’s biweekly newsletter featuring global gospel stories and encouragement from David Platt and others to help you follow Jesus and make him known. Subscribe today .",
   "content_zh": "因此,我为主而遭俘虏,求你按照你们被召唤的礼仪而行走。 (第4:1页) 这些话很重 他们的体重不是因为保罗从安静的书房或舒适的地方写作,而是因为他从监狱牢房里写作。 保罗不会像世界所定义的那样, 他在写作时, 对他的批评者来说,他是一个麻烦制造者. 一个危险的煽动者。 对上帝来说,他是一个忠实的仆人。 这很重要。 他敦促以弗所的教会记住并活下来。 他的话很重,因为他的生活支持他们。 他并没有要求他们做他自己没有做的事, 保罗的惊人之处是:他没有失败的可能。 他将前往地底, 宣扬基督, 将他关起来,他将成为“主的俘虏”,将他的痛苦变成福音价值的活生生的见证。 处决他? 精细。 正如他在别处所说的那样,“对我来说,是基督,而死亡是利益”(Phil. 1:21,ESV)。 保罗说,“福音已经不够了。 ” 他没有一刻可以得出结论,“这不值得。 ” 相反,保罗的整个生活,特别是他的痛苦,证明了这一真理:无论你的情况如何,相信福音永远不会白白浪费。 保罗并未入狱, 他入狱是因为他认为这个消息值得忍受. 这是一个重要的区别。 如果信息不值得痛苦, 基督教不会保证没有痛苦的生活。 它所许诺的是更好的事情:没有意义的痛苦。 希望苦难得不到最终结果。 这就是为什么保罗可以在两个科林斯语中说:“由于这种短暂的灾难,我们准备了一种永恒的光荣之分,超越一切的比喻,因为我们不看所见的事物,而看所见的事物。 所见之物是短暂的,而所见之物是永恒的。” 福音为我们提供了一个忍受暂时痛苦的框架,因为它将我们置于永久的承诺中。 现在我们可以忍受苦难, 我们的疼痛有结束期 为什么? 因为耶稣已经在我们的位置上遭受了完全的痛苦。 他自受其害、死亡、判决。他从坟墓中走出, 因为他,连死亡都无法战胜我们 当保罗敦促信徒们“以值得召唤的方式行走”时, 他邀请他们过着充满希望的生活, 这篇文章首次刊登在委员会的双周通讯《激进》上, 今日订阅.",
   "image": "https://cdn.radical.net/images/20261030060443/Prison-Didnt-Mean-Paul-Was-Wrong.png",
