  {
   "title": "Standing with Brothers and Sisters We Cannot See",
   "url": "https://radical.net/article/brothers-sisters-cannot-see/",
   "category": "missionary",
   "source": "Radical (David Platt)",
   "source_url": "https://radical.net/feed/",
   "published": "2026-11-11",
   "summary": "David Platt shares a powerful story of forgiveness and challenges Christians to support persecuted believers through prayer.",
   "summary_zh": "大卫·普拉特（ David Platt ）分享了一个关于宽恕的强大故事，并挑战基督徒通过祈祷来支持受迫害的信徒。",
   "title_zh": "与我们看不到的兄弟姐妹站在一起",
   "content": "I remember the first time I stood at the demilitarized zone, or the DMZ, on the border between North and South Korea. I looked across this dividing line, knowing that on the other side I (and we) have brothers and sisters who are suffering and dying for following Jesus and making him known. I think about Soon Yangown, a pastor in a leper colony during the Japanese occupation of the Korean peninsula many years ago. He refused to worship the shrines that had been set up for the Korean people to worship, so he was arrested and imprisoned. When the Japanese occupation ended, he was released. Years later, his two oldest sons, Tongin and Tongsin, were in middle school when a mob representing the communist party came to their campus. The boys were known to be strong Christians, so the mob brought them out of the school and began to beat them. Eventually, a leader of the mob named Ahn shot and killed both the boys. By the time Pastor Soon heard of his sons’ death, the revolt had been quieted, and his sons’ killer had been apprehended. Upon hearing this news, Pastor Soon immediately sent a messenger to the court to plead for them to spare Ahn. The judge agreed to do so, and Pastor Soon then offered to adopt Ahn as his own son. Years later, communists invaded the leper colony where Pastor Soon was pastoring. He refused to flee and chose to stay with the church. He was arrested, imprisoned, tied up with 75 other followers of Jesus, and executed. I wish these stories were only from the past. Unfortunately, however, suffering, persecution, and martyrdom are present-day realities for our brothers and sisters in North Korea. So what can we do for them? For starters, we can remember them and learn about what they are experiencing. In the words of Hebrews 13:3: “Remember those who are in prison, as though in prison with them, and those who are mistreated, since you also are in the body.” We can take time to listen to and learn the story of our family in North Korea as if we were with them in North Korea. (Radical’s documentary Hard to Reach: North Korea is a good place to start.) As you remember our brothers and sisters in North Korea and other hard lands , I invite you to pray for them. Let your prayers be guided by Paul’s exhortation in Ephesians 6:18–20 for the church to pray for him while he was in prison: “To that end, keep alert with all perseverance, making supplication for all the saints, and also for me, that words may be given to me in opening my mouth boldly to proclaim the mystery of the gospel, for which I am an ambassador in chains, that I may declare it boldly, as I ought to speak.” Finally, let’s join them. They’re giving their lives to share the gospel with people around them. If you’re reading this, the likelihood is that your life is not at stake in the same way as theirs. But you can still give your life to what they’re giving their lives to: speaking the gospel and sharing God’s love in Jesus with the people you and I interact with today. Let’s lock arms with our brothers and sisters in North Korea , following Jesus and making him known no matter what it costs, because we know that he is our reward. This article first appeared in The Commission , Radical’s biweekly newsletter featuring global gospel stories and encouragement from David Platt and others to help you follow Jesus and make him known. Subscribe today .",
   "content_zh": "我记得我第一次站在非军事区,即位于南北朝鲜边境的非军事区。 我越过了这个分界线,知道在另一边,我(和我们)有兄弟姐妹,他们因追随耶稣并让他出名而受苦受难并死去. 我想起在多年前日本占领朝鲜半岛期间,一个麻风病殖民地的牧师Soon Yangown。 他拒绝为朝鲜人民所崇拜而设置的神社,于是被捕入狱. 日本占领结束后被放出. 多年后,他的两个最年长的儿子通仁和通辛在中学就读,当时一个代表共产主义政党的暴徒来到了他们的校园. 男孩们已知是坚固的基督徒,于是暴徒将他们从学校带出并开始殴打他们. 最终,一个暴徒的领袖命名 安开枪打死了两个男孩 在Sonon牧师听到他儿子的死讯时,叛乱已经平息,杀害他儿子的凶手已被逮捕。 在听到这个消息后,索恩牧师立即派使者去朝廷恳求他们放过安. 法官同意这样做,而索恩牧师随后提出收养安为自己的儿子. 多年后,共产主义分子入侵了\"索恩牧师\"正在作牧师的麻风病人聚居地. 他拒绝出逃,选择留在教堂. 他被逮捕,入狱,被绑起与另外75名耶稣追随者,被处死. 我希望这些故事只是从过去。 然而,不幸的是,对我们北朝鲜的兄弟姐妹来说,痛苦、迫害和殉道是当今的现实。 我们能为他们做什么? 首先,我们可以记住他们,了解他们正在经历的一切。 用希伯来人13:3的话来说:“记住那些在狱中的人,好像在狱中一样,以及那些受到虐待的人,因为你也是在身体里。” 我们可以花时间聆听和学习我们家在朝鲜的故事,就像我们在朝鲜与他们在一起一样. (Radical的纪录片\"难去难去\":朝鲜是个好起点. ) 我邀请你为他们祈祷。 保罗在《以弗所书6:18-20》中劝告教会在狱中为他祈祷:“为此,请全心全意地为所有圣人祈祷,也为我祈祷,愿为我大胆地开口宣讲福音之谜,因为我是一个被锁住的大使,让我大胆地宣示福音之谜。 最后,让我们加入他们。 他们献出生命与周围的人分享福音。 如果你读到这个, 你的生命可能与他们的生命不同。 你仍然可以把生命献给他们所付出的:说出福音,与你我今天交往的人分享上帝对耶稣的爱。 让我们和朝鲜的兄弟姐妹们握起手来, 这篇文章首次刊登在委员会的双周通讯《激进》上, 今日订阅.",
   "image": "https://cdn.radical.net/images/20261026061606/Standing-with-Brothers-and-Sisters-We-Cannot-See-featured-image.jpg",
