  {
   "title": "How Missionaries Should Serve Where Churches Already Exist",
   "url": "https://radical.net/article/missionaries-serve-churches-exist/",
   "category": "missionary",
   "source": "Radical (David Platt)",
   "source_url": "https://radical.net/feed/",
   "published": "2026-11-08",
   "summary": "Brian Pate explains how missionaries can equip national churches to mature, reproduce, and plant healthy churches for generations.",
   "summary_zh": "布莱恩·佩特（ Brian Pate ）解释了传教士如何让国家教会成熟、繁殖和种植健康的教会。",
   "title_zh": "传教士应该如何在教会已经存在的地方服务",
   "content": "I’m a missionary in Brazil , and I’m surrounded by churches—Baptist, Presbyterian, Nazarene, Assembly of God—the list goes on and on. Brazil has a rich history of missionaries who left a legacy of churches that are now sending missionaries to other countries. This raises a question: Should we send missionaries to countries where churches already exist? Shouldn’t we consider those regions “reached” and send our resources into more needy areas of the world? It’s a fair question, and worth exploring. Before we land on an answer, here are a few factors to consider: REMEMBER THE EXAMPLE OF PAUL Didn’t Paul say his mission was to reach those who had never heard the gospel (Rom 15:20)? Yes, a significant part of Paul’s ministry was as a church-planting missionary. But he did not abandon these newly planted churches. Paul was also a church-strengthening missionary. He revisited the churches he planted (Acts 14:21–23; 15:36; 18:22–23). He wrote letters to these churches because there was more that he needed to teach them (1 Thess 3:10). He even sent out his own missionary workers to areas he had previously reached: Tychichus to Ephesus and Colossae, Titus to Corinth, Crete, and Dalmatia, and Timothy to Ephesus and other cities. Surprisingly, we don’t have any record of Paul sending missionaries to unreached areas, but only to areas he had already reached. Paul creates helpful categories for us. We see Romans 15 pioneer missions, but we also see Acts 14 church-strengthening missions. Should we send missionaries where churches already exist? Paul shows us there is a biblical category for missions like this . In fact, Paul did it. CONSIDER THE HEALTH OF THE CHURCHES As I have trained pastors over the years, I have encountered alarming situations: churches that tolerate immorality, pastors who fail to understand the simple gospel message, and church leaders who unknowingly regurgitate heresy to their congregation. In my denomination, many churches are either embracing prosperity theology or depending on pragmatism to fulfill the Great Commission . Yes, I’m surrounded by “churches”, but when we only consider truly evangelical churches, the list is much smaller. And if we want to further whittle it down to healthy churches, the number gets very slim. Consider the health of the churches. If we neglect “reached” fields, we run the risk of creating new unreached people groups in just a few generations. UNDERSTAND THE NEEDS OF GOSPEL INFRASTRUCTURE So, what is the role of a missionary in a “reached” country? It is helpful to consider the missionary’s role in the big picture of what God is already doing in that country. This is where the rubric of gospel infrastructure is so helpful. Think of it this way: A city needs to put certain structures in place (such as road networks, water supply, and energy grids), so that it can continue to exist and thrive in the future. Similarly, the Great Commission requires far more than initial evangelism. For a national church to grow and reproduce, it will need to have “gospel infrastructure” in place. This begins with evangelism, Bible translation, and planting healthy churches, but also includes Christian literature, local pastor training, local worship music, and seminaries. The missions task involves everything a national church needs, so they can continue planting healthy churches for generations to come. When sending missionaries to areas that already have churches, we need to ask where the missionary’s gifts and the national church’s needs intersect. For example, Brazil probably does not need missionaries to plant more churches, but there are still great needs in the areas of seminaries and local pastor training. MISSIONS WHERE CHURCHES ALREADY EXIST? Should we send missionaries where churches already exist? Based on Paul’s own example, the answer is yes. We should continue sending Great Commission workers anywhere there is the need for healthy churches and gospel infrastructure. May we joyfully serve in",
   "content_zh": "我在巴西是传教士, 我周围是教堂, 巴西有丰富的传教士历史,他们留下了现在向其他国家派遣传教士的教会遗产. 这提出了一个问题:我们是否应该派遣传教士到已经存在教堂的国家去? 我们不应该认为那些地区已经“到达”了, 这是一个公平的问题, 在回答之前, 有几个因素需要考虑: 记得PAUL的ExAMPLE吗? 对,保罗部相当一部分是教会的传教士。 但他并没有放弃这些被新建的教堂. 保罗也是一名加强教会的传教士. 他重新审视了所种植的教堂(第14:21-23;15:36;18:22-23号法案)。 他给这些教堂写了信,因为他需要更多的教他们(1 Thess 3:10). 他甚至派遣了自己的传教工人去他之前所到的地区:提奇丘斯去以弗所和克洛斯塞,提图斯去科林斯,克勒特去和达尔马提亚去,提摩西去以弗所等城市去. 令人惊讶的是,我们没有任何保罗派遣传教士前往未到达的地区的记录,而只是前往他已经到达的地区。 保罗为我们创造了有用的类别。 我们看到罗马人的15个先锋任务,但我们也看到14号行动加强教堂的任务。 我们是否应该派传教士到已经存在教堂的地方? 保罗向我们展示了一个圣经类的任务。 事实上,保罗做到了。 考虑中国的健康 这些年来,我训练了牧师,我遇到了令人震惊的情况:教会容忍不道德,牧师不理解简单的福音信息,教会领导人不知不觉地把异端归罪于他们的教会。 许多教会要么接受繁荣神学, 但当我们只考虑真正福音派教会时, 如果我们想把它进一步切入到健康的教堂里, 数字变得非常微弱。 考虑教会的健康。 如果我们忽视“接触”领域,我们就有可能在短短几代人中产生新的未接触人群。 理解政府基础设施的需要 因此,传教士在“传入”国家的作用是什么? 在大背景下考虑传教士对上帝在该国已经采取的行动的作用是有益的。 这是福音基础设施的特色 如此有帮助。 这样想:一个城市需要建立某些结构(如道路网、供水和能源网),以便它能够继续存在并在未来兴起。 同样地,大委员会要求的远远不止于最初的福音. 国家教会要发展和再生,就必须建立“神道基础设施”。 这开始于福音会,圣经翻译,并种植健康的教堂,但也包括了基督教文学,地方牧师培训,地方崇拜音乐,和神学院. 任务涉及国家教会需要的一切,因此他们可以继续为后代种植健康的教堂. 当派遣传教士前往已经拥有教堂的地区时,我们需要问传教士的礼物和全国教会的需求在哪里交叉. 例如,巴西可能不需要传教士来种植更多的教堂,但在神学院和地方牧师培训方面仍有巨大的需要. 任务 Churches已经存在 我们是否应该派传教士到已经存在教堂的地方? 根据保罗自己的例子,答案是肯定的。 我们应继续派遣大委员会的工人到任何需要健康教堂和福音基础设施的地方去。 愿我们愉快地服务于",
