  {
   "title": "The Importance of Non-Instrumental Activity",
   "url": "https://mbird.com/everyday/the-importance-of-non-instrumental-activity/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=the-importance-of-non-instrumental-activity",
   "category": "lay",
   "source": "Mockingbird",
   "source_url": "https://mbird.com/feed/",
   "published": "2026-11-03",
   "summary": "The Secret Hobbies of Mike Tomlin (and Bryan Jarrell)   The post  The Importance of Non-Instrumental Activity  appeared first on  Mockingbird .",
   "summary_zh": "Mike Tomlin （和Bryan Jarrell ）的秘密爱好The post The Importance of Non-Instrumental Activity首次出现在Mockingbird上。",
   "title_zh": "非仪器活动的重要性",
   "content": "Here’s something that retired Pittsburgh Steelers head coach Mike Tomlin and I have in common: we both have secret hobbies that we don’t talk about. My hobby is houseplants. About two years ago, I decided I needed a hobby that was affordable, indoors, incapable of being monetized, offered zero status, and could be managed while raising two preschool-aged kids. The result was a half-priced money tree purchased off the clearance rack from Lowe’s. It was touch and go at first: two of the four “trunks” braided together on this money tree were dying of root rot, and it took me a while to figure out the right lighting and a good watering schedule. (Did you know that most houseplants don’t die from lack of water but from being overwatered? The more you know!) Once I figured all that out, my money tree stabilized, and a new hobby was born. About twelve years ago, the then head coach of the Pittsburgh Steelers, Coach Tomlin, started playing Minecraft with his two preteen sons. For the unfamiliar, Minecraft is like a digital game of Legos, and while there are competition elements to the game, Coach Tomlin felt the draw of the game’s free-play mode, which allowed gamers to build their own creations out of the game’s various styles of bricks. Earlier this fall, having moved from coaching football to broadcasting, Tomlin disclosed his twelve-year Minecraft hobby to the world, offering a YouTube live stream tour of a city that he had spent more than a decade building from digital bricks. Both gamers and sports fans responded in mutual shock. If you’re not an NFL or sports fan, you might not know that Mike Tomlin is one of sport’s most respected head coaches, famously stringing together nineteen years of coaching without a single losing season. He won the Super Bowl in 2009 and kept the Steelers among football’s most winning franchises. One of his talents was keeping the sport’s most “diva” players in winning form. When top-flight players would leave the Steelers for other teams, many would go on to have public crashouts and exit the league in embarrassing failures. Players uniformly loved Tomlin, and many viewed him as a surrogate father figure. But when the Sunday Night Lights were off, and when the family was cared for, and when the man had some free time, the famous head coach was on his laptop playing Minecraft. This is as unusual as it is amazing. Professional sports of all stripes are marked by an “all-in” mentality. Everything is secondary to the game, whether it’s diet, exercise, sleep, even family and friendships. The money is just too much and the stakes are too high to be anything but 100% focused on preparing for the game ahead. And yet, Coach Tomlin achieved his incredible results while spending a lot of time hanging out in the land of the chicken jockey. Gamers have been just as shocked as the football fans. When Tomlin hosted his YouTube live stream of his city, it garnered universal appreciation from other Minecraft players. It was big, well designed, and orderly down to every last detail and accent. In the great divide between the nerds and the jocks, here was a jock who could keep up with the nerds. Arthur Brooks, the social science good-life guru, had lots to say about Tomlin’s hobby over at the Free Press . He confessed to being “gobsmacked” at the news and took the moment to reflect on his own lack of hobbies. Sparing you the requisite “here’s what a bunch of scholarly studies found about hobbies” bits that populate Brooks’ articles, here’s what stood out: A key element of a hobby that brings happiness is that the rewards must not be extrinsic. By this, I mean the hobby can’t run up your personal or professional scoreboard in terms of money, power, or prestige. The rewards must be intrinsic, involving personal growth, enjoyment, or drawing closer to others. Psychologists have long found a huge happiness difference between these two types of reward. For example, in one famous study from 2009, researchers at the Universit",
   "content_zh": "我和迈克·汤姆林有一个共同点:我们都有秘密的爱好, 我的爱好是家用植物 大约两年前,我决定我需要一种爱好,这种爱好是负担得起的,室内的,无法被货币化,提供零地位,可以在抚养两个学龄前儿童的同时进行管理.\n\n结果是一棵半价钱树从Lowe的清关架上购买。 起初是触摸并去的:在这棵钱树上被捆绑在一起的4个\"trunks\"中有2个正被根腐烂而死,我花了一阵子才找出正确的照明和良好的取水时间表. (你知道大多数家用植物不是因为缺水而死,\n\n越发知道! ) 一旦我明白了这一切, 我的钱树稳定下来了, 一个新的爱好诞生了。 大约12年前,时任匹兹堡钢人队主教练的汤克林教练开始和他的两个小儿子一起出演\"Minecraft\".\n\n对于不熟悉的人来说,\"Minecraft\"就像一款\"Legos\"的数字游戏,虽然游戏中存在竞争元素,但主教练汤姆林感受到了游戏自由游戏模式的图画,这使得游戏者能够从游戏的各种花砖风格中打造出自己的创作.\n\n今年秋天早些时候,汤姆林从教练足球转向广播,他向世界披露了自己十二年的\"Minecraft\"爱好,提供YouTube活溪游览一个他用数字砖建造了十多年的城市. 比赛双方球迷和体育迷均在相互冲击下响应.\n\n如果你不是NFL或体育迷, 你可能不知道迈克·汤姆林是体育界最受尊敬的主教练之一, 他赢得了2009年的超级杯,并将钢人队保留在了足球最赢的特许赛中. 他的才华之一是让体育界最能\"跳出\"的选手保持获胜的状态.\n\n当顶级飞行的球员会离开钢人队前往其他球队时,许多人会继续公开出场并因尴尬的失败而退出联赛. 玩家一致地爱戴了托姆林,许多人将他视为代父人物.\n\n但当\"星期日夜光\"停用后,当家人被照顾后,当男方有一些闲暇时间时,出名的主教练正在他的笔记本电脑上玩\"Minecraft\". 这和不可思议一样不寻常 各行各业的体育运动都带有“全能”的心态。 无论是饮食、锻炼、睡眠、甚至是家庭和友谊,\n\n这笔钱太高了,赌注也太高了,除了百分之百地专注于为前面的比赛做准备之外,什么都做不了. 然而,教练汤姆林却取得了他令人难以置信的成绩,同时花了很多时间在鸡骑师的地上闲逛. 赌徒们和足球迷一样震惊\n\n当汤姆林主持他所在城市的YouTube直播流时,获得了其他Minecraft玩家的普遍好评. 它很大,设计精良,并且有序地下降到每一个细节和口音. 在书呆子和游手好闲的鸿沟中,这里有一个可以跟上书呆子的游手好闲.\n\n社会科学好生活大师亚瑟·布鲁克斯(Arthur Brooks), 他供认在新闻上被“污辱”,并借此机会反思自己缺乏爱好。\n\n给大家打个比方说 : “ 这是一堆关于爱好的学术研究发现的 ” , 这些研究充斥着布鲁克斯的文章, 突出之处是: 带来幸福的爱好的一个关键要素是,奖励决不能是外生的。 我是指爱好不能在金钱、权力或声望方面,\n\n奖励必须是内在的,涉及个人成长、享受或接近他人。 心理学家长期发现这两种奖励之间有着巨大的幸福差异. 例如,在2009年的一项著名研究中,大学研究人员",
   "image": "",
   "author": "Bryan Jarrell",
   "tags": [
    "Everyday",
    "Featured",
    "Sports",
    "Arthur Brooks",
    "hobbies",
    "Homebrewing",
    "Houseplants",
    "MIke Tomlin",
