  {
   "title": "Rumors of Grace",
   "url": "https://mbird.com/religion/testimony/rumors-of-grace/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rumors-of-grace",
   "category": "lay",
   "source": "Mockingbird",
   "source_url": "https://mbird.com/feed/",
   "published": "2026-11-03",
   "summary": "What Mbird Has Been for Me   The post  Rumors of Grace  appeared first on  Mockingbird .",
   "summary_zh": "Mbird对我来说是什么恩典的帖子谣言首先出现在知更鸟上。",
   "title_zh": "恩典的谣言",
   "content": "“You only work on Sundays, anyway.” One of the go-to jokes of a group of guys I hang out with. I laugh because, well, I just like to please people. But every time the boys say it, I think to myself, “If you only knew what being a pastor feels like sometimes.” An impromptu funeral at the clinic for veterinarians whose colleague committed suicide, scrolling to find an angry email about last week’s sermon or the changing of the carpet color in the narthex, begging financial generosity from people who pay your salary, lunch with a lonely man going through a brutal divorce, restocking the youth fridge with 7,000 Cheerwines, hearing that they’re gossiping about you again, failing yet again to prepare a sermon that marries TikTok and TED Talk, off to the shelter to serve dinner, did I even eat lunch? Did I mention that this was last Tuesday? Ministry is often pure gift. The highs are as high as they get. Pastors bear witness to the most intimate, most raw, most human, most beautiful and terrifying moments of people’s lives. We play at the level of the soul. We get paid to read and write and pray. We have a front-row seat to miracles: the bound freed, the sinner forgiven, the debts paid, the dead resurrected. It’s not lost on me that having a job rife with meaning is a privilege and honor. This is what God has chosen to make of my life, and I am truly grateful. And yet, there’s no way to get away from the fact that ministry is a burden too. Its shape is a cross. Its form is death and resurrection. People bring all their junk to church — and they should. Sunday mornings are not an assembly of the righteous, they are a sanctuary for sinners — hypocrites anonymous, I like to call it. One of the chief problems that has arisen in my ministry is that I find it so hard to be ministered to. Ministers minister, preachers preach, consolers console, but if you’re not careful, a pastor will find herself without a pastor, a preacher will find himself without a preacher. Everyone needs both. Not long ago I burnt out. Years after, I realize the burnout was not the product of overwork. The fire in my soul was extinguished because for years I had offered relief to others without finding ways to receive it myself. What becomes of a healer who forsakes his own wounds? Pay attention when your soul stops singing. And I did. I prayed for God to bring relief, somehow, someway. And then, to quote Morgan Freeman in The Shawshank Redemption , “a beautiful little bird flew into my drab little cage.” That beautiful bird was Mockingbird Ministries. And now for six years I have done what Michael Gershon calls “staking one’s life on the rumors of grace.” As the 300th of the Mockingcast draws near, I thought it fitting to say what Mbird has been for me: My theological home: Pastors in the mainline will tell you that our denominations are fracturing. It’s tempting to give in to extremes and to weaponize politics toward ecclesial ends. This has made me feel like I don’t belong anywhere politically, theologically, or pastorally. I know many fellow pastor Mbirders agree because we’ve talked about it. Pastors in the mainline who long for a ministry rooted only in the gospel are like an island of misfit toys. Mbird has become for us a gracious home and a place to be reminded again and again of the gospel of grace. My Preacher: I listen to the Mockingcast on Monday mornings. You pastors out there know Monday’s malaise. Dizzied and frenzied from all of Sunday’s work and pressured by a million regrets about the sermon or the attendance, I find sweet relief in the words of RJ, Sarah, and Dave. “That’s right,” I think. “This is what ministry about.” God may not speak to us all through an audible voice in our heads, but God does speak through the words of a sermon. On Mondays, the Mockingcast is the sermon I need to hear. And just like my own parishioners, after I hear the good news, I find myself ready for another week. My Storyteller: We are narrative beings. Ask Jesus. He wa",
   "content_zh": "“不管怎样,你只在星期天工作。” 我和一群人一起玩的笑话 我笑是因为我喜欢取悦别人 但是,每当男孩们说,我想,“如果你知道牧师有时会是什么感觉。 ” 在诊所为兽医举行即兴葬礼, 其同事自杀, 滚动寻找愤怒的电子邮件, 有关上周的布道或地毯颜色的改变, 我有提到这是上周二吗? 礼部常为纯礼. 高取相如来相. 牧师见证了人们生活中最亲密、最生动、最人性化、最美丽和最恐怖的时刻。 我们在灵魂的层面演奏。 我们得到报酬读写祈祷. 我们有一个前排的座位 奇迹: 束缚的释放者, 罪人原谅, 债务偿还, 死人复活。 工作充满意义, 这是上帝选择 使我的生活, 我真的很感激。 然而, 也没有任何办法可以摆脱这个事实, 其形状为十字. 它的形式是死亡和复活. 人们把他们所有的垃圾都带到教堂,他们应该这样做。 星期天早晨不是善人集会,而是罪人的避难所——伪君子匿名,我想称之为. 在我部里出现的主要问题之一是,我感到很难处理。 但是如果你不小心, 牧师会发现自己没有牧师, 每个人都需要两者。 不久前我烧焦了 多年之后,我意识到 燃烧不是过度劳累的结果。 我灵魂中的活火被扑灭了,因为多年来我一直向其他人提供救济,但没有找到自己接受的方法. 一个抛弃了自己伤口的医护人员会怎样? 你的灵魂停止唱歌时要注意 我这样做了。 我祈求上帝带来解脱,不知何故,某种方式。 然后,引用摩根·弗里曼(Morgan Freeman)在\"肖申克救赎\"(The Shawshank Resemption)中的话,“一只美丽的小鸟飞入了我的小兔子笼子。” 那美丽的鸟是鸟类部 而现在,六年来,我做了迈克尔·格申所说的“用恩典的谣言夺走自己的生命 ” 。 随着\"摩克活人\"第300期的到来,我认为应该说出Mbird对我的意义: 我的神学家:主线的牧师会告诉你,我们的教派正在分化. 将政治武器化, 这让我觉得自己不属于政治、神学或牧业的任何地方。 我知道许多牧师都同意, 主线的牧师渴望一个只植根于福音的部,就像一个不适应玩具的岛屿. Mbird已经成了我们一个善良的家 以及一个可以被反复提醒的恩赐之地 我的传教士:周一早上我听口语广播. 你们这些牧师知道星期一的病情。 我发现RJ、Sarah和Dave的言语令人欣慰。 \" 没错, \" 我认为。 “这是部委的旨意。” 上帝可能不会通过我们脑海中的声音向我们大家说话,但上帝确实通过布道的话说话。 周一,嘲笑者是 我要听到的布道。 就像我的教区教友一样 在我听到好消息后 我发现自己已经准备好再待一个星期了 我的童话故事:我们是叙事生物。 问耶稣。 他来了",
