   "published": "2026-11-06",
   "summary": "When sorrow and suffering take you by the hand / Going no contact? Ruth and faithful presence / Why Christians don’t have to fear the collapse / Does evangelicalism exist? / Reformation Day myths / Best practices from evangelistically effective churches See Also A La Carte (July 23) A La Carte (May 30) A La Carte (May 16)",
   "summary_zh": "当悲伤和痛苦牵着你的手/不接触？路得和信徒的存在/为什么基督徒不必害怕崩溃/福音派存在吗？/宗教改革日神话/传福音有效教会的最佳实践另请参阅单点菜肴（ 7月23日）单点菜肴（ 5月30日）单点菜肴（ 5月16日）",
   "title_zh": "周末点菜（ 10月31日）",
   "content": "Good morning. My gratitude goes to Ligonier Ministries for sponsoring the blog this week to ensure you know about the new Grow app . It’s a free download and offers a lot of great content. Sales & Deals Today’s Kindle deals include a variety of deals spanning a bunch of different eras and genres. Recommended Reading Jessica Lee writes about the growing no contact movement in which people sever their relationships with people they find toxic or problematic. I think of the young women I mentor who are flooded with reels, encouraging them that the best way to deal with the strained relationship with their mom is to put up higher boundaries that limit contact, or to cut off contact altogether. Even if they don’t take these drastic measures, they cannot help being influenced by the ideas, and neither can we. We are steeped in a therapy culture that talks a lot more about self-care than self-sacrifice, and we would be naive if we didn’t acknowledge the formative influence it has on us. And so, when relationships (whether with a parent, sibling, or friend) get hard, as they inevitably will, our first impulse is often toward self-preservation (putting up higher boundaries) and not self-sacrificial presence and faithfulness. Read: Going No Contact?: Ruth and Faithful Presence. Madeline Livingston has been going through a time of deep sorrow, and from that perspective writes a meditation about grief and glory. In my grief, I can turn to a Savior who has known human frailty and deep suffering, a Savior who in his most desperate moment prayed, “Let this cup pass from me. Yet not as I will, but as you will” ( Matthew 26:39 ). I can trust in the goodness and provision of a Father who did spare his own Son, but gave him up to rescue us and make us his own ( Romans 8:32 ). I can rest in the compassion of a Maker who collects all my tears in his bottle ( Psalm 56:8 ), a Good Shepherd who carries me safely through the lowest valley and restores my soul ( Psalm 23 ). As Sorrow and Suffering take me by the hand, they invite me to run to the solid ground of God’s promises made yes and amen through Christ. They urge me on to seek solace in the only place it can truly be found, in a arms of a Father who is my “refuge and strength, a very present help in trouble” ( Psalm 46:1 ). Read: When Sorrow & Suffering Take You by the Hand. Sarah Eekhoff Zylstra did a lot of interviews to try to arrive at some best practices for churches that are evangelistically effective. Her article offers a lot of food for thought. Thirty years ago, evangelical churches leaned on programs like Evangelism Explosion or Continuous Witness Training to deliver evangelistic focus and training, church leader Thom Rainer said. Those structured initiatives fit well in a culture that was more Christian and more institutionalized—it took an average of 20 church members to reach one person for Christ per year. Today, it takes 50, Rainer said. “Evangelism efficacy, evangelism effectiveness, has definitely diminished.” That trend is likely to continue. As social media and AI fill our time and increase our social anxiety, even relational evangelism is growing more difficult. Read: Best Practices from Evangelistically Effective Churches. Like Darryl Dash, I’ve been hearing and reading a lot of doom and gloom lately, much of it related to the rise of AI. But also like Darryl, I don’t feel particularly alarmed. I read quite a few predictions of doom over the weekend, including some from self-professed tech optimists. Supposedly, some infrastructure in the United States is under attack. The idea is that AI is becoming too powerful and will overwhelm whatever safeguards we have in place to protect things like our water systems and electricity. The proposed solution is to prepare for the disaster by buying generators, solar panels for generators, and stockpiling water and other basic supplies. However, the authors acknowledge that there is little we can do to truly protect ourselves from the da",
   "content_zh": "早上好,你好吗? 感谢利戈尼耶各部赞助本周的博客, 这是免费的下载, 今天的销售和交易包括一系列跨越不同时代和流派的交易。 建议阅读 Jessica Lee写道,人们与他们发现有毒或有问题的人们断绝关系,但这种接触运动却在不断增加。 我想到那些我辅导的年轻女性,她们被卷轴淹没,鼓励她们,处理与母亲的紧张关系的最佳方式是设置更高的界限限制接触,或者完全切断接触. 即使他们不采取这些激烈的措施,他们也受不了这些想法的影响,我们也不能这样做。 我们身处一种治疗文化中,谈论自我护理比自我牺牲要多得多,如果我们不承认它对我们的形成影响,我们就会天真。 因此,当关系(无论是与父母、兄弟还是朋友的关系)变得困难时,我们的第一个冲动是往往朝向自我保护(提出更高的界限),而不是自我神圣的存在和忠诚。 读:去去不接触? 鲁斯和忠实的存在。 马德琳·利文斯通经历了一段深深的悲痛时期,从这个角度看,他写了一篇关于悲痛和荣耀的冥想. 在我悲痛中,我可以求助于一个知道人类脆弱和深重苦难的救世主,一个在他最绝望的时刻祈祷的救世主,“让这个杯子从我身边流过。 然而,不是我愿意,而是你愿意”(Matthew 26:39.). 我可以相信一个父亲的善良和供养,他确实饶了自己的儿子,但为了拯救我们而放弃了他,使我们成为自己的父亲(罗马8:32. 我可以安心地忍受一个造物主的怜悯,他把所有的眼泪都收在他的瓶中(诗篇56:8),一个好牧人,他把我安全地带入最底层的山谷并恢复我的灵魂(诗篇23. 当悲伤和受苦将我牵上手时,他们邀请我跑到上帝通过基督许下的承诺的坚实地步。 他们敦促我在一个真正能找到的地方寻求安慰,在一个父亲的怀抱中,他是我的“抵抗和力量,在困难中非常现成的帮助”(第46:1段)。 读取:\"悲伤\"和\"受苦受难\"由\"手取\"\"你\"时. Sarah Eekhoff Zylstra做了很多访谈,试图为福音派有效的教堂达成一些最佳做法. 她的文章为思考提供了很多食物. 教会领袖汤姆·赖纳(Thom Rainer)说, 这些结构化的倡议非常适合一种更加基督教化和更加制度化的文化——平均每年需要20名教会成员为基督而接触一个人。 今天,它需要50,雷纳说。 “福音会的功效,福音会的功效,肯定已经下降。” 这一趋势很可能继续下去。 由于社交媒体和AI填补了我们的时间并增加了我们的社会焦虑,甚至连关系福音派也越来越困难. 读取:Evangelistic 有效教会的最佳做法。 与达里尔·达什(Darryl Dash)一样, 我最近也听到并阅读了许多末日与忧郁, 但与达里尔一样, 我读到很多关于周末末日的预言, 包括一些自以为是的技术乐观主义者的预言。 假设美国的一些基础设施受到攻击。 这个想法是AI正在变得太强大,并且会压倒我们现有的保护诸如我们的供水系统和电力之类的东西的保障措施. 拟议的解决办法是通过购买发电机、发电机太阳能电池板以及储存水和其他基本用品来为灾难做好准备。 然而,作者们承认,我们无法真正保护自己,使其免受歧视。",
