  {
   "title": "IMB missionaries share vision for Seoul during Serve Tour",
   "url": "https://www.imb.org/2026/10/01/imb-missionaries-share-vision-for-seoul-during-serve-tour/",
   "category": "missionary",
   "source": "IMB",
   "source_url": "https://www.imb.org/feed/",
   "published": "2026-11-05",
   "summary": "Ask most people what comes to mind when they think of South Korea, and they’ll likely mention K-pop, skincare, cutting-edge technology, or bustling city streets filled with young professionals. Hun and Eunjoo Sol see those things too. But after more than a decade serving as International Mission Board missionaries in Seoul, they also see something many visitors never notice. “Many people think that Korea is exciting and successful,” Hun said. “They think about the K-pop, the K-culture, K-technol",
   "summary_zh": "他们可能会提到K-pop、护肤、尖端科技, 或是充满年轻专业人士的城市街道。 匈奴和恩朱·索尔也看到了那些东西. 但是,在担任国际使团委员会在首尔的传教士十多年后,他们也看到了许多游客从未注意到的事情. “许多人认为韩国是令人兴奋和成功的,”Hun说。 “他们想到的是K-流行、K-文化、K-科技。",
   "title_zh": "IMB传教士在服务之旅中分享首尔的愿景",
   "content": "Ask most people what comes to mind when they think of South Korea, and they’ll likely mention K-pop, skincare, cutting-edge technology, or bustling city streets filled with young professionals. Hun and Eunjoo Sol see those things too. But after more than a decade serving as International Mission Board missionaries in Seoul, they also see something many visitors never notice. “Many people think that Korea is exciting and successful,” Hun said. “They think about the K-pop, the K-culture, K-technology, K-cosmetics. Yes, good things… But many Korean people are struggling right now.” The Sols, who were featured in the 2025 Week of Prayer for International Missions , continue to rely on the prayers of faithful Southern Baptists. Prayers sustain them and help to push back the darkness they confront in their home culture. Hun often describes that hidden struggle with a Korean word: han. There is no simple English translation. It speaks to a deep sorrow shaped by generations of war, division, loss, and the quiet resolve to endure. Hun sees it in the young adults he meets every week—people navigating loneliness and relentless expectations, quietly asking what their lives are for beneath a veneer of success. Han is heavy. For many young adults, that weight can feel unbearable. Suicide is one of the leading causes of death among teenagers and young adults in South Korean. Hun and Eunjoo see the effects of that reality every day, and it’s one reason they believe they have no time to waste. That understanding shaped the week of Serve Tour Seoul this summer. There were no construction sites, no paintbrushes, no tools or work gloves. Instead, the team stepped into ministries local churches had been faithfully building long before the volunteers arrived. In May, 27 volunteers from seven churches across the United States came alongside local churches already serving their communities in Seoul, Paju, and Gwangju. They taught English classes, ran children’s camps, walked neighborhoods in prayer, distributed food, and sat down with college students, one conversation at a time. For Nick Holst of Sandals Church in Riverside, California, the goal was simple enough to say in a sentence: “We came to meet people,” Holst said. “We want to hear their stories, and that leads to some very meaningful connections as you get past the surface into something deeper.” That’s where the week actually lived: past the surface and past the small talk, where the quiet weight of han is often carried without words. On college campuses, volunteers played games, asked questions and mostly just listened. Some conversations lasted a few minutes and ended there. Others kept going long past the point where either person expected them to. One morning, after asking his church back home to pray, Holst sat down with a university student named Intec. They talked about culture first, the way strangers do, and then the conversation turned somewhere neither of them planned. “I got to ask him, ‘What do Koreans believe?’” Holst recalled. Intec said he didn’t believe in God. Then he recited John 3:16 from memory, word for word, a verse he’d heard somewhere along the way and never forgotten. Before they parted, Holst asked if he could pray that God would keep drawing Intec toward Himself. “He said yes.” In a city where many conversations never move beyond the surface, the exchange was a reminder of why the team had come. Not to win an argument, but to listen, to ask questions, and to trust God with what happens next. The same pattern unfolded elsewhere, beginning in classrooms and ending on city streets. Jeremy Doyle, missions pastor at Valley Baptist Church in Bakersfield, California, watched an English conversation club grow from two students on its first night to eight by the second. Nobody advertised it as anything more than a place to practice English, but the room kept filling, and so did the conversations. The team’s work with students didn’t stop there. At a nearby Christian schoo",
   "content_zh": "他们可能会提到K-pop、护肤、尖端科技, 或是充满年轻专业人士的城市街道。 匈奴和恩朱·索尔也看到了那些东西. 但是,在担任国际使团委员会在首尔的传教士十多年后,他们也看到了许多游客从未注意到的事情. “许多人认为韩国是令人兴奋和成功的,”Hun说。 “他们思考的是K-流行、K-文化、K-技术、K-化妆品。 对,好东西... 但许多朝鲜人民目前正在挣扎。” 在2025年国际传道会礼拜周中亮相的索尔人继续依靠忠实的美南浸信会的祈祷. 祈祷会支持他们,帮助推倒他们在家乡文化中面对的黑暗. 匈奴经常用韩语来形容这种隐性的斗争:han. 没有简单的英文翻译。 它代表着代代相传的战争、分裂、损失和宁静的忍耐决心所形成的深刻的悲痛。 匈奴从他每周相遇的青壮年中看到它——人们在探索孤独和无情的期望,悄悄地问着他们在成功之光下的生活是什么. 汉为重. 对于许多年轻的成年人来说,这种体重可以感到难以忍受. 自杀是韩国青少年和年轻成年人死亡的主要原因之一. 匈奴和恩朱每天都看到现实的影响, 这一理解决定了今年夏天的首尔旅游周。 没有建筑工地,没有油漆刷,没有工具或工作手套。 相反,小组在志愿人员抵达之前很久就进入了各部的地方教堂。 5月,来自全美7所教堂的27名志愿者与已经在首尔,帕珠和光州为社区服务的地方教会一起来. 他们教授英语课程,管理儿童营地,走近邻里祈祷,分发食物,并与大学生坐下来,一次交谈一次. 对于加利福尼亚州里弗赛德的桑达尔斯教堂(Sandals Church)的尼克·霍尔斯特(Nick Holst)来说,这个目标非常简单,在一句话中说:“我们是来见人的,”霍尔斯特说。 “我们想听到他们的故事,这会导致一些非常有意义的联系,当你从地表走入更深处。” 这周实际上就住在那里:经过地表,经过了小说,汉语的静静重量经常被无言地携带. 在大学校园里,志愿者们玩游戏,提问并大多只是听. 有些谈话持续了几分钟,到此结束. 其他人则一直远走高飞, 一天早晨,霍尔斯特在请他回到家乡的教堂祈祷后,与一位叫因特克的大学生坐下来. 他们首先谈论文化, 和陌生人一样, 然后对话转身的地方 他们都没有计划。 “我要问他,`韩国人相信什么? ' 霍尔斯特召回。 Intec说他不相信上帝。 然后他从记忆中背诵了John 3:16, 在他们离开之前,霍尔斯特问他能否祈祷上帝会继续将Intec画给自己. “他说是。” 在一个许多对话从未超越表面的城市里,交流提醒了球队来的原因. 不取一辩而取之,而听之取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取而取 同样的模式在其他地方出现,从教室开始,到城市街道结束. 杰里米·道尔(Jeremy Doyle)是加利福尼亚州贝克斯菲尔德的Valley浸信会教会的传教士,他看着一个英语对话俱乐部从第一个晚上的两个学生成长到第二个晚上的八个. 没有人宣传它只是一个练习英语的地方,但房间一直填满,对话也一样. 团队与学生的合作并未就此而止. 在附近的一个基督教会",
