  {
   "title": "A La Carte (November 3)",
   "url": "https://www.challies.com/a-la-carte/a-la-carte-november-3-2026/",
   "category": "giant",
   "source": "Tim Challies",
   "source_url": "https://www.challies.com/feed/",
   "published": "2026-11-10",
   "summary": "May God be gracious to us: a prayer before Election Day / A wretch like me / On the naming of death / Readers respond on Elisabeth Elliot’s sexuality / The belly dancer grandmas / Looksmaxxing: the normalizing of vanity / Kindle deals, new books, and more. See Also Weekend A La Carte (December 27) A La Carte (October 2) Kindle Deals for Christian Readers",
   "summary_zh": "愿上帝保佑我们:选举日前的祈祷。 在死亡的命名上, 肚皮舞奶奶 / Lookmaxing: 虚荣的正常化 / Kindle 交易,新书等. 又见周刊A La Carte(12月27日) A La Carte(10月2日) Kindle Deals for Christian Readers.",
   "title_zh": "点菜（ 11月3日）",
   "content": "Grace and peace to you, my friends. I’ve got a list of great links for you to consider reading today, then a brief look at some of this week’s new Christian books (plus a link to discover many more). Sales & Deals Today’s Kindle deals are excellent! Concise Reformed Dogmatics is a great deal, since the list price is $40. Also consider Nancy Guthrie’s devotional resource to help you pray for your children. The Discipline of Grace is one of my all-time favorite books. Over at Westminster Books, you can get the best pricing on Dissecting the Heart , a book that “brings some of David Powlison’s best insights to a new generation that is struggling to deal with its relationship to secular psychology and spiritual formation.” Recommended Reading May God Be Gracious to Us: A Prayer Before Election Day. As America heads to the polls, Christian voters may benefit from reminding themselves of the truths in this article and praying the prayer it provides. “On May 17, 1776, John Witherspoon stepped into the pulpit at Princeton on a day of fasting the Continental Congress had appointed for all the colonies. War had already begun and independence was less than two months away. Witherspoon, a Presbyterian minister and soon-to-be signer of the Declaration of Independence, called the occasion a ‘day of solemn humiliation.’ Before he said a word about the conflict, he turned his hearers’ eyes to the sovereign providence of God. He closed with a prayer: ‘God grant that in America true religion and civil liberty may be inseparable, and that the unjust attempts to destroy the one, may in the issue tend to the support and establishment of both.'” Looksmaxxing: The Normalizing of Vanity. Jacob Crouch: “To say it plainly, Christians don’t looksmaxx. However much we may be into fitness or beauty, we reject looksmaxxing on three levels: our worth, our temporality, and our purpose. Our beauty is temporary; and all our beauty is for His glory. Our beauty does not determine our worth, because our value is fixed in Christ.” A Wretch Like Me. This is a very well-written article about John Newton and the reason he chose to describe himself as a wretch. “When Newton wrote what would become one of the best-loved hymns of the Christian church, he was looking back on a life that had taken him to rock bottom – and into the horrors of the Atlantic slave trade. Reading ‘Amazing Grace’ is like witnessing a confession. You are invited into someone’s life, and its darkest parts are laid bare before you. But the hymn doesn’t only dwell on the wretchedness of sin; it celebrates the deep, satisfying transformation of its author. And what a transformation it was!” The Belly Dancer Grandmas. This writer tells about the unfortunate experience of seeing belly-dancing grandmothers and relates it to his experiences on the frontiers of missions. “I remember once seeing a Facebook post from a Melanesian friend, commenting on an American pro-transgender news report. A direct translation of her comment would be, ‘Look at this – the whiteskins have lost their minds!’ Now, it wasn’t that long ago, perhaps even as recently as her great-grandparents’ generation, that her own society was a cannibalistic one, trapped in its own temporary insanity.” Thankfully, God so often interrupts that temporary insanity. On the Naming of Death. Andrew Roycroft has a strong and moving article about the naming of death. “In this short piece, I want to reflect on our obsession and squeamishness about the grotesqueness of death and to suggest some ways our current season of the year might reveal our deeper cultural preoccupations. In a follow-up article, I want to think about the practice of naming the dead, and the importance of measured and authentic memorial.” Reader Responses to ‘What We Know—and Cannot Know—About Elisabeth Elliot’s Sexuality’. Christianity Today ran several responses to the recent article about Elisabeth Elliot’s sexuality, including one by my friend Nick. “Let me be clear: I do not",
   "content_zh": "祝你们平安 朋友们 我有一个伟大的链接清单, 供大家考虑今天阅读, 然后简要地看看本周的一些基督教新书(再加上发现更多书的链接). 今天的销售和交易是极好的! 简明改革的Dogmatics是一个很大的,因为列表价格是40美元.\n\n也考虑Nancy Guthrie的奉献资源, Grace的纪律是我最喜欢的书之一\n\n在威斯敏斯特书院,你可以拿到\"解剖心灵\"的最佳定价,这本书“将大卫·波利森的一些最佳见解带给新一代人,他们正在努力处理其与世俗心理学和精神形成的关系。 推荐阅读愿上帝保佑我:选举日之前的祷告.\n\n当美国领导投票时,基督教选民可能会从提醒自己这篇文章的真相和祈祷它所提供的祈祷中获益. \" 1776年5月17日,约翰·威瑟斯庞在禁食大陆大会任命所有殖民地的一天,踏入普林斯顿的船舱. 战争已经开始,独立还不到两个月。\n\nWitherspoon是一位长老会的大臣, 在他谈到冲突之前,他把听觉者的眼睛转向上帝的主宰。\n\n他最后祷告说:“真主允许在美国,真正的宗教和公民自由是不可分割的,不公正的破坏这种自由的企图可能倾向于支持和确立两者。” Lookmaxing:虚荣的正常化. Jacob Crouch:“直截了当地说,\n\n无论我们多么喜欢健身或美貌,我们反对在三个层面的外观:我们的价值、时间和目的。 我们的美丽是暂时的;我们所有的美丽都是为了他的荣耀. 我们的美丽并不决定我们的价值,因为我们的价值固定在基督中。” 像我这样的坏蛋\n\n这是一篇关于约翰·牛顿和他选择把自己描述成一个卑鄙小人的原因的非常精心撰写的文章. “当牛顿写下将成为基督教教堂最受爱戴的赞美诗之一时,他回顾曾经把他带到最底层的生活,以及大西洋奴隶贸易的恐怖。 阅读“惊人的恩典”就像见证忏悔。\n\n你们被邀入他人的生活中,而其中最黑暗的部分被揭穿。 赞美诗不仅停留在罪恶的悲惨之中, ” 贝利舞者奶奶\n\n这位作家讲述了看到肚皮舞祖母的不幸经历,并将其与他在任务前沿的经历联系起来。 我曾经看到美拉尼西亚朋友的Facebook文章,\n\n她的评论直接译为:\"看这个——白人已经疯了!\" 现在,也许早在她曾祖父母这一代人之前,她自己的社会就是一个吃人的社会,被困在自己的暂时精神错乱之中。 谢天谢地,上帝经常打断这种暂时的疯狂。 论死名.\n\n安德鲁·罗伊克罗夫特(Andrew Roycroft)有一篇强烈而动人的文章,内容涉及死亡的命名. “在这篇短文中,我想反思我们对死亡的迷恋和惊恐,并提出一些可能揭示我们更深层次的文化关切的方法。\n\n在一篇后续文章中,我想考虑点名死者的做法,以及有计量和真实纪念的重要性。” 读者回应「我们所知道的, 包括我的朋友尼克(Nick), “让我说清楚:我没有",
