  {
   "title": "Building a Workplace Where People Flourish (with Landon Hobson)",
   "url": "https://www.thegospelcoalition.org/podcasts/your-corner-of-the-world/building-workplace-people-flourish/",
   "category": "devotional",
   "source": "The Gospel Coalition",
   "source_url": "https://www.thegospelcoalition.org/feed/",
   "published": "2026-11-11",
   "summary": "What happens when a seminary-trained economist takes over a pet-products plant? Landon Hobson, CEO of Cosmos Corporation, shares how paid mission days, a book club, and a ‘make the coffee’ ethic help ordinary workers flourish.",
   "summary_zh": "当受过神学院培训的经济学家接管宠物产品工厂时会发生什么？ Cosmos Corporation首席执行官兰登·霍布森（ Landon Hobson ）分享了带薪传教日、读书俱乐部和“制作咖啡”伦理如何帮助普通工人蓬勃发展。",
   "title_zh": "打造让人们茁壮成长的工作场所（与Landon Hobson一起）",
   "content": "Landon Hobson is the CEO of Cosmos Corporation, a family business outside St. Louis that makes pet care and household cleaning products sold in 70 countries. With degrees in theology and economics, Landon never expected to run a 250,000-square-foot manufacturing plant—until his father-in-law asked him to take over the company, and he sensed that God had been preparing him for exactly that. Landon describes how his faith shapes Cosmos’s culture: tuition support for any degree an employee wants to pursue, a “Better Book Club” that pays people to read, five paid mission days a year, and a core value of being “willing to make the coffee” for the person behind you. He answers hard questions about whether making dog shampoo really matters to God, and he speaks candidly about the hard years—supply chain disruptions, a drop in sales, and layoffs of people he cared about. He also explains why CEOs need pastors who aren’t passive, and what he has gained from a group of believing business leaders who share the same struggles. Resources Mentioned: To Change the World by James Davison Hunter Every Good Endeavor by Timothy Keller A Public Faith by Miroslav Volf Work in the Spirit by Miroslav Volf",
   "content_zh": "兰登·霍布森(Landon Hobson)是宇宙公司(Cosmos Corporation)的首席执行官,该公司是圣路易斯以外的一家企业,在70个国家销售宠物护理和家庭清洁产品. 凭借神学和经济学的学位,兰登从未预期会经营25万平方英尺的制造厂——直到岳父要求他接管公司,他感到上帝一直在为他做好完全这样的准备.\n\nLandon描述了他的信仰如何塑造宇宙文化:为雇员想要追求的任何学位提供学费支持,一个支付人们阅读费的“更好的书社”,每年有5个付薪的任务日,以及为背后的人“愿意煮咖啡”的核心价值。\n\n他直言不讳地说, 连锁供应中断、销售量下降、以及他所关心的人被解雇。 他也解释了为什么CEO需要不是被动的牧师,\n\n资源参考:改变世界 詹姆斯·戴维森·亨特 每一个好努力 蒂莫西·凯勒 公共信仰 米罗斯拉夫·沃尔夫 在精神中的工作 由米罗斯拉夫·沃尔夫",
   "image": "https://media.thegospelcoalition.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/28151928/Ep3-Thumbnail-YT-YCOTW.png",
   "author": "Mark Vroegop, Dan Doriani, Landon Hobson",
   "tags": [
    "Podcast",
    "Faith and Work"
   ],
   "language": "en",
   "country": "US",
   "region": "",
   "stance": "evangelical",
   "flags": [],
   "priority": 2,
   "fetched_at": "2026-11-11T00:25:53.226792",
   "extra": {
    "feed_type": "rss20"
   },
   "id": "7c7f584352238a0f"
  },
  {
   "title": "The Missing Piece in John Mark Comer’s Quiet Time",
   "url": "https://www.thegospelcoalition.org/reviews/into-quiet-noise/",
   "category": "devotional",
   "source": "The Gospel Coalition",
   "source_url": "https://www.thegospelcoalition.org/feed/",
   "published": "2026-11-10",
   "summary": "‘Into the Quiet’ effectively critiques the ills of our hectic cultural moment, but it also promotes a spirituality that could quickly become disconnected from Scripture.",
   "summary_zh": "“进入宁静”有效地批评了我们忙碌的文化时刻的弊端，但它也促进了一种可能很快与圣经脱节的灵性。",
   "title_zh": "约翰·马克·科默安静时光中缺失的部分",
   "content": "According to a recent Pew Research study, more than half of U.S. adults report spending too much time on their smartphones. Even those who acknowledge the negative effects of smartphone addiction find it incredibly difficult to cut back. Excessive screen time isn’t just harming our productivity and connection with other people; it also makes practicing spiritual disciplines more challenging. In his latest book, Into the Quiet: Following Jesus in a World of Noise , John Mark Comer argues that “nurturing a practice of solitude, silence, and stillness is utterly indispensable to a flourishing spiritual life” (26). This timely book addresses important issues in a lively, engaging, and accessible manner. And though the staccato prose style sometimes borders on gimmicky, it’s never dull. Comer dons three hats, appearing as technoskeptic, therapist, and mystic. I responded differently to each: cheering the first, querying the second, and rejecting the third. Into the Quiet effectively critiques the ills of our hectic cultural moment, but it also makes questionable claims about the spiritual value of silence and promotes a spirituality that could quickly become disconnected from Scripture. Comer as Technoskeptic Comer’s consistent message is that readers need to intentionally disconnect from an increasingly noisy world. By “noise,” he means both literal sounds and the ever-present flood of distraction enabled by digital devices. The result is that “it is now possible to go through our entire lives and never be alone ” (18, emphasis original). Channeling secular authors like Jonathan Haidt and Johann Hari, Comer argues that constant noise threatens our well-being, whether we’re religious or not. But this dynamic is especially harmful for Christians. “Are we really surprised that . . . when we sit down to pray, we can barely even pay attention?” he asks (20). Our always-on lives differ greatly from the example of Jesus, who models a strategy of “retreat and return” (30). He ministers to the crowds and then withdraws to solitary places to pray (e.g., Matt. 14:23). This is the book’s strongest material, a timely warning with which I wholeheartedly agree. Comer as Therapist But then Comer moves in another direction. He advances therapeutic claims that may be helpful to some people but that aren’t clearly grounded in Scripture. According to Comer, solitude forces a confrontation with pain that serves an essential psychological purpose: All of this stuff is in you . . . leaking out like toxic pollution into the water supply of your relational world . . . it’s asking to be dealt with. To be given a moment to speak, say its piece. To be healed. Released. (85) While the imprecatory psalms and the psalms of lament certainly model honest prayer before the Lord, and self-examination is clearly a biblical imperative, Comer’s specific claims seem more informed by extrabiblical therapeutic practices than by Scripture itself. Is it actually true that “naming our feelings out loud allows our bodies to process and discharge pain without it getting lodged in our cores” (100)? That doesn’t sound like Scripture. According to Comer, solitude forces a confrontation with pain that serves an essential psychological purpose. The primary biblical support he gives for the practice of naming feelings to discharge pain is Jesus’s prayer in the garden of Gethsemane, an incident that provides the “template” for “the moments of our own Golgothas” (101). The problem is that Jesus in the garden isn’t surfacing old “toxic pollution” from “the water supply of [his] relational world” with an eye toward therapeutic healing (85). He’s facing an imminent crisis and asking the Father for either deliverance from or strength to face the trial set before him. Whatever the value of surfacing past trauma, Gethsemane doesn’t establish Comer’s claims on that point. Comer as Mystic It will be unsurprising for those who have followed Comer’s work, but he also emphasizes a form of mysti",
   "content_zh": "根据近期的皮尤研究研究,超过半数的美国成年人报告说在智能手机上花费了太多的时间. 即使是承认智能手机成瘾负面影响的人 也觉得很难减少 过度的屏幕时间不仅会损害我们的生产力和与其他人的联系,\n\nJohn Mark Comer在其最新著作《静默:在噪音的世界中追随耶稣》中认为,“折磨一种孤独、沉默和静静的习俗对于繁荣的精神生活是绝对不可或缺的”(26)。 这本及时的书以活泼、参与和无障碍的方式处理了重要问题。 虽然这种散文风格有时会与 gi相接,\n\n来者会戴三顶帽子 以技术怀疑者、治疗师和神秘主义者的身份出现 我对每一个都作出了不同的反应:欢呼第一个,询问第二个,拒绝第三个.\n\n静悄悄地有效地批评了我们繁忙的文化时刻的弊端,但也提出了关于静悄悄的精神价值的令人怀疑的主张,并提倡一种精神性,这种精神性可能会很快脱离圣经. 读者需要有意与日益吵闹的世界断绝联系。\n\n他所说的“噪音”既指文字上的声响,又指数字设备所引发的无时无刻不在的分散注意力。 其结果是,“现在有可能经历我们的整个生活,从不孤独......”(18,强调原文)。 comer指出, 不断的噪音威胁着我们的福祉, 无论我们是否信奉宗教。\n\n但这种动态对基督徒特别有害. “当我们坐下来祷告时,我们甚至几乎不能注意,我们真的感到惊讶吗?” 我们始终不渝的生活与耶稣的榜样大不相同,耶稣树立了“遣返和返回”战略(30)。 他向人群派遣大臣,然后撤往单独地点祈祷(如马特14:23).\n\n这是这本书最强的材料, 来者作为治疗师, 但随后,来者向另一个方向移动。 他提出治疗性要求,\n\n据Comer说,孤独迫使与痛苦的对峙为基本的心理目的服务: 所有这些东西都在你体内, 渗出像有毒污染一样, 渗入你关系世界的水供应中, 得到时间说话,说说它的片段。 以被疗之. 释之.\n\n(85) 虽然圣诗和哀叹的圣诗无疑是上帝面前的模范诚实祷告,而且自我检查显然是圣经的必经之义,但比起《圣经》本身,考美尔的具体主张似乎更能从圣经外的治疗做法中得到启发。\n\n事实上,“大声点出我们的感情,使我们的身体能够处理和释放痛苦,而不将痛苦放在我们的核心中”(100)。 这听起来不像圣经。 据Comer说,孤独迫使一种与痛苦的对抗,这为基本的心理目的服务.\n\n耶稣在Gethsemane的花园里祈祷,这是他为“我们自己的Golgothas(101)时刻”提供“时代”的主要圣经支持。 问题在于花园里的耶稣并没有从“关系世界的供水”中冒出老“有毒污染”,\n\n他正面临迫在眉睫的危机, Gethsemane并没有证明Comer对此的主张。 来者如神秘 对于跟随Comer的作品的人来说,这不会令人惊讶,但他也强调一种神秘的形式.",
   "image": "https://media.thegospelcoalition.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/24211209/into-quiet-noise-1.jpg",
   "author": "Matthew Bingham",
   "tags": [
    "Christian Living",
    "Reviews",
    "Biblical Authority",
    "Psychology and Disorders",
    "Sanctification and Growth",
    "Technology",
    "Union with Christ"
   ],
   "language": "en",
   "country": "US",
   "region": "",
   "stance": "evangelical",
   "flags": [],
   "priority": 2,
   "fetched_at": "2026-11-10T02:32:47.327513",
   "extra": {
    "feed_type": "rss20"
   },
   "id": "35c3f51acba08346"
