  {
   "title": "Peace for Second-Guessers",
   "url": "https://rss.desiringgod.org/link/10732/17513407/peace-for-second-guessers",
   "category": "devotional",
   "source": "Desiring God",
   "source_url": "https://www.desiringgod.org/blog.rss",
   "published": "2026-11-05",
   "summary": "How do we know when we’ve worked hard enough? Pastor John offers six ways to fight anxiety and rest in the grace of Christ. Listen Now",
   "summary_zh": "我们如何知道我们已经足够努力了？约翰牧师提供了在基督的恩典中战胜焦虑和安息的六种方法。现在聆听",
   "title_zh": "Peace for Second-Guessers",
   "content": "Audio Transcript Last week, we looked at whether Pastor John was trained well enough to be a pastor when he became one in 1980. We live in a world where you can get a lot of formal training, and it can be very helpful. But all the training in the world will not alleviate the anxiety we can feel over whether we are serving people adequately. “Imposter syndrome,” some call it, or “perfectionism.” We all want to excel at our callings. We want to be capable. We want to succeed. And we want to bless others and truly impact the world for good. So, how do we learn to leave off the anxiety that says we could do everything better? That’s today on Ask Pastor John: peace for second-guessers. An anonymous man, a surgeon, writes with our question today: “Pastor John, thank you for this podcast, and for sharing your own experience as an aspiring medical professional in college, which I have heard you mention in APJ episodes 1373, 1382, 1730, and 1916 at least. I relate to that part of your story as a surgeon currently struggling with anxiety and stress related to my job. Many nights, I lie awake, worrying about my patients, whether I could have made better decisions, how I could improve my skills, or stressing over unresolved issues. “This constant drive to do more and be better makes me less present with my family, and sometimes I even yearn for early retirement. While I understand that God is sovereign over my patients and their outcomes, I also know that my own effort and diligence directly impact their care. My question is: How do I find the proper balance between resting in God’s providence and being diligent in my job, without becoming so consumed by my work that it pulls me away from my family when I’m off the clock? These competing forces are causing me inner turmoil, and I’m struggling to navigate them well.” Faith-Filled Effort That question is absolutely basic to living the Christian life, and it’s not unique to the pressures of being a surgeon. It’s an issue for teachers who want to have good lessons for their students, and for the handyman who wants his repair job not to break down the day after he leaves. So, did he do a good enough job? Did he spend enough time on it? For pastors who wonder if they’ve made enough house calls, done enough evangelism, prepared long enough on the sermon. For moms who wonder, Have I spent enough time with my kids? For consultants who wonder if they’ve put in enough hours to be worthy of the fees they’re collecting, and on and on and on. In other words, everybody who is a serious Christian faces the question, “In view of God’s sovereignty and wisdom and goodness in my life, have I done enough, or have I done too much? Am I exerting faith-filled effort to do the best job I can? Too much or too little?” Everybody faces this question. It’s a wonderful, so-important question. Our surgeon friend says, “I worry whether I could have made better decisions and how I could improve my skills.” He knows something’s off because he’s often not really present with his family when he’s present with his family. He asks, “How can I find the right balance . . . resting in God’s providence and being diligent in my job?” In the end, what I would say to start with is this: Peace of mind like that is a gift of God. It’s a fruit of the Holy Spirit. It goes beyond human rationality and figuring out. We know that because Paul says that in Philippians 4:7: “the peace of God, which surpasses all understanding.” That’s amazing. He knows you can’t figure this out. You can’t make peace happen by your rational computations. That peace will guard your hearts and minds in Christ Jesus, though it comes from him directly through means but is not entirely quantifiable with your reason. It goes beyond human rationality. So, I pray now that this gift will be given to this surgeon, and everybody else who’s listening who struggles with this — like all of us. Six Ways to Fight for Peace But I know that God uses his word often in ways that",
   "content_zh": "上星期,我们研究了约翰牧师是否受过足够好的训练,在1980年成为牧师时,他是否成为牧师. 我们生活在一个你可以接受很多正式训练的世界,这可以很有帮助. 但是,世界上的所有培训都无法减轻我们对我们是否在为人民充分服务感到的焦虑。\n\n“冒牌综合症,”有人称之为“完美症”。 我们都想出色地履行我们的使命。 我们希望有能力。 我们希望取得成功。 我们想祝福他人,真正地影响世界。 那么,我们如何学会摆脱那些 说我们可以做得更好的焦虑? 今天,\n\n一位匿名人士,一位外科医生,今天用我们的问题写道:“约翰神父,谢谢你的播客,感谢你把自己作为大学有抱负的医学专业人员的经验分享给大家,我听到你在APJ第1373、1382、1730和1916集至少提到了这一点。 我指的是你作为外科医生的那部分 与我的工作有关的焦虑和压力\n\n很多晚上,我睡着, 担心我的病人, 是否我能做出更好的决定, 如何提高我的技能, “这种不断努力做更多的事情,做更好的事情,使我更不愿意与家人在一起,有时甚至渴望提前退休。\n\n虽然我知道上帝对我病人及其结果拥有主权,但我也知道,我自己的努力和勤奋直接影响到他们的护理。 我的问题是:我如何在安息于上帝的保佑和勤奋地工作之间找到适当的平衡,而不会因为我的工作而变得如此消耗,以至于我下班时会远离家人?\n\n这些相互竞争的力量正在引起我内心的动荡, 充满信仰的努力 这个问题对于基督教生活来说是绝对最基本的,\n\n这对想为学生上好课的老师来说, 那么,他做得够好吗? 他花足够的时间吗? 在布道上准备了足够长的时间。\n\n给那些好奇的妈妈们 我和孩子们在一起的时间够多了吗? 这些顾问想知道他们是否投入了足够的时间, 换句话说,每个认真的基督徒都面对一个问题,“鉴于我生命中上帝的主权、智慧和善良,我做得够不够,还是做了太多?\n\n我是否在尽我所能 尽我最大的努力? 太多还是太少?” 每个人都面临这个问题。 这是一个奇妙而重要的问题。 我们的外科医生朋友说 , “ 我担心我是否能够做出更好的决定以及如何提高我的技能。 ” 他知道有些事情是错的,因为他经常在家人在场时不真正与家人在一起。\n\n他问:我怎样才能找到正确的平衡呢?我只依真主的恩典而谨守我的工作。 最后,我首先要说的是:心灵和平是上帝的礼物。 这是圣灵的果实。 它超越了人类的理性 和思考。\n\n我们知道这一点,因为保罗在菲利普派4:7中说:“上帝的和平,它超越了一切理解。” 这是惊人的。 他知道你不能明白这一点。 你无法通过理性的计算实现和平。 这种和平将保护你们在基督耶稣中的心灵和心灵,尽管它来自他直接通过手段,但并没有用你的理由完全量化.\n\n它超越了人类理性. 所以,现在我祈祷,这个礼物会送给这位外科医生, 以及所有那些正在听话的人, 像我们所有人一样。 六种为和平而战的方法 但我知道上帝经常用他的话",
   "image": "https://dg.imgix.net/resources/17915/media/f2647d27-9624-4fe4-8547-00bdf7e94442/landscape/peace-for-second-guessers-en.jpg?ts=1791218677&ixlib=rails-4.3.1&fit=crop&w=2000&h=1050",
