  {
   "title": "The Pull of Rome and the Promise of Grace",
   "url": "https://rss.desiringgod.org/link/10732/17512004/the-pull-of-rome-and-the-promise-of-grace",
   "category": "devotional",
   "source": "Desiring God",
   "source_url": "https://www.desiringgod.org/blog.rss",
   "published": "2026-11-06",
   "summary": "A member of your small group confesses, seemingly out of the blue, that he’s been attending Mass. Next, you hear a deacon wonder aloud if we really needed to break with Rome. Then a family member announces that she has “crossed the Tiber,” meaning she has converted to Roman Catholicism.   Such conversations seem to be happening with greater frequency in Protestant circles. But why?   Ask around, and you’ll get a dozen different answers: a hunger for apostolic authority, textured liturgy, histori",
   "summary_zh": "也承认参加弥撒。 接下来,你听到一个执事 大声问我们是否真的需要 与罗马决裂。 然后,一位家庭成员宣布,她“跨越了台伯河”,这意味着她已皈依罗马天主教。 这种对话似乎在新教圈子里更频繁地发生。 为什么?\n\n四处打听,你会得到十几个不同的答案:对叛教权威的渴望、纹理仪式、神话",
   "title_zh": "罗马的引领和恩典的承诺",
   "content": "A member of your small group confesses, seemingly out of the blue, that he’s been attending Mass. Next, you hear a deacon wonder aloud if we really needed to break with Rome. Then a family member announces that she has “crossed the Tiber,” meaning she has converted to Roman Catholicism. Such conversations seem to be happening with greater frequency in Protestant circles. But why? Ask around, and you’ll get a dozen different answers: a hunger for apostolic authority, textured liturgy, historical rootedness, and tangible grace. Also, a distaste for consumerism, hyper-individualism, and a fragmented and culturally irrelevant church. It’s usually a pull and a push, and almost always a long time in the making. As we consider the specific factors animating Romeward journeys, it’s helpful to understand the dynamics that motivate the process of conversion — in both directions. Vortex of Vulnerability Lewis Rambo, in his seminal work Understanding Religious Conversion , suggests that conversion begins with a destabilizing event. The crisis could be the loss of a loved one, career disappointment, cultural upheaval, or a sense that life no longer coheres. All it takes is one crisis to create “vulnerability,” an existential openness to a new way of living. Several arriving together, though, create an emotional and intellectual current (a vortex, in Rambo’s nomenclature), pulling one toward a new perception of reality. A few years back, I enjoyed a public dialogue at Hillsdale College with a Roman Catholic scholar who had been raised evangelical Protestant. As a young man studying the classics, he saw a cleavage (or perhaps a yawning chasm) between the early church and the faith of his own congregation. For such a precocious young man, this was a genuine crisis that prompted his departure from Protestantism. His decision, however, wasn’t limited to his ecclesial angst. It unfolded gradually and involved a host of experiences that deepened his discontent and piqued his desire for what Rome offered. Of course, this vortex works in both directions — conversion to Rome or from it. Consider my story. I was raised in an Italian Roman Catholic family and worked professionally in Catholic dioceses across the country. Growing up on Long Island, all my friends were Roman Catholic (or Jewish). Protestantism was foreign to me, and I had no interest in it. If you own a set of Big Bertha golf clubs, why bother with the imitation knockoffs? But then I had a crisis. The story is long and complicated, but an illness brought me to my knees. I feared for my life and my soul. I lacked assurance — if I died, would I open my eyes to see God’s smile? It was then, suddenly, that several Protestant friends walked into my life, all with the same message: “By yourself, Chris, there’s no hope. Turn from your sin and embrace Christ alone for salvation, as the Savior who died for you, who rose, and who now lives.” I believed, and everything changed — including my church. And as with my Catholic friend at Hillsdale, all of this took time, unfolding through months of questions: Who am I? Why am I here? What can I trust? Current Trends Research today suggests more people are leaving the Roman Catholic Church than are joining it. Pew’s 2023–24 Religious Landscape Study , the latest one available, finds that for every convert Catholicism receives, roughly eight adults raised Catholic no longer identify as such, and about 15 percent of those raised Catholic now identify with a Protestant tradition. The data do not yet capture the reverse flow, that is, how many Protestants become Catholic. Several US dioceses have reported notable increases in adult converts since 2024, though it’s too early to know whether this represents a lasting shift. To many, though, the dominant movement seems to be toward Rome, especially if you follow the issue online. Spend an evening on YouTube and you’ll find an entire ecosystem devoted to Catholic apologetics: conversion testimonies, debates, fo",
   "content_zh": "也承认参加弥撒。 接下来,你听到一个执事 大声问我们是否真的需要 与罗马决裂。 然后,一位家庭成员宣布,她“跨越了台伯河”,这意味着她已皈依罗马天主教。 这种对话似乎在新教圈子里更频繁地发生。 为什么?\n\n四处问一问,你会得到十几个不同的答案:对叛教权威的渴望、纹理性、历史根源和有形的恩典。 而且,对消费主义、超个人主义和支离破碎、文化无关的教会感到厌恶。 这通常是一种拉力和推力,\n\n在我们考虑罗马之旅的具体动画因素时,了解推动转变过程的动态——双向变化——很有帮助。 在他开创性的作品《理解宗教皈依》中,\n\n危机可能是失去爱人、事业失望、文化动荡,或感觉生活不再和谐。 只要一场危机,就会产生“脆弱性”,一种对新生活方式的生存开放。 然而,一些人走到一起,创造了一种情感和智力的潮流(在兰博的名词中是漩涡),拉动了对现实的新认识。\n\n几年前,我在希尔斯代尔学院享受了与一位罗马天主教学者的公开对话,这位学者被培养为福音派新教徒. 作为一个学习经典的年轻人,他看到了早期教会和他自己会众信仰之间的裂痕(或可能是打哈欠的裂痕). 对于这样一个早熟的年轻人来说,这是一场真正的危机,促使他脱离了新教.\n\n然而,他的决定并不限于他的愤怒。 它逐渐展开,并涉及大量的经验,加深了他的不满情绪,并激发了他对罗马所提供事物的渴望. 当然,这种漩涡在双向作用——皈依罗马或从罗马. 考虑我的故事。\n\n我在一个意大利罗马天主教家庭长大,在全国天主教教区从事专业工作. 在长岛长大,我的朋友都是罗马天主教(或犹太教). 新教对我而言是外来的,我对此毫无兴趣. 如果你有一套Bertha高尔夫球俱乐部, 为什么要费心 模仿的淘汰赛? 但后来我遇到了危机。\n\n故事既长又复杂,但疾病让我跪下。 我担心生命和灵魂 我缺乏保证——如果我死了,我会睁开眼睛看上帝的笑容吗? 突然间,几个新教朋友走进我的生活,他们都带着同样的信息:“克里斯,你自己,没有希望了。\n\n求你从你的罪恶中转过身来,求你只为拯救而拥护基督,象为你们而死的救世主那样,他复活了,他现在活着。” 我相信,一切都改变了——包括我的教堂。 就像我在希尔斯代尔的天主教朋友一样 所有这一切需要时间 经历几个月的问题: 我是谁? 为什么我在这里? 我还能相信什么?\n\n目前的趋势研究显示,离开罗马天主教会的人数比加入教会的人数多。 皮尤的2023–24年宗教景观研究(最新研究)发现,对于每一个皈依天主教的人来说,大约8名成年人的天主教徒不再被确认为天主教徒,而现在,大约15%的天主教徒认同新教传统。\n\n这些数据还不能反映逆流,即有多少新教徒成为天主教徒. 一些美国教区报告说,自2024年以来,成人皈依人数明显增加,尽管现在知道这是否代表了持久的转变还为时过早。 但对于许多人来说,主导运动似乎是朝罗马的方向发展,特别是如果你在网上关注这个问题。\n\n在YouTube上过一个晚上, 你会发现整个生态系统都专注于天主教的学说:转换证词、辩论、fo",
   "image": "https://dg.imgix.net/resources/17831/media/8c96cda9-19ec-4679-8777-0fdcc7006e6f/landscape/the-pull-of-rome-and-the-promise-of-grace-en.jpg?ts=1791225384&ixlib=rails-4.3.1&fit=crop&w=2000&h=1050",
   "author": "Chris Castaldo",
