   "id": "49873f22249fadf8"
  },
  {
   "title": "A Daring Suggestion for Netflix",
   "url": "https://www.challies.com/articles/a-daring-suggestion-for-netflix/",
   "category": "giant",
   "source": "Tim Challies",
   "source_url": "https://www.challies.com/feed/",
   "published": "2026-11-11",
   "summary": "Netflix appears to have fallen on hard times and I have some suggestions that I'd like to think would help it: cut the sex, nudity, and profanity, and gain back the viewers it's long been alienating. See Also A La Carte (April 28) A La Carte (October 22) Netflix’s Biggest Competition",
   "summary_zh": "Netflix似乎陷入了困境，我提出了一些我认为会有所帮助的建议：减少性、裸露和亵渎，并赢回长期以来一直疏远的观众。另请参阅点菜（ 4月28日）点菜（ 10月22日） Netflix最大的比赛",
   "title_zh": "对Netflix的大胆建议",
   "content": "Netflix appears to have fallen on hard times. The company’s co-CEOs recently announced they would be laying off around 1,000 employees, the deepest round of cuts in years. A day later, the chief content officer made a sudden exit and was immediately replaced. Just a week after that, the market responded unfavorably to Netflix’s Q3 results, causing the stock to drop sharply. There are many reasons for Netflix’s woes, not the least of which are increasingly heavy competition for viewers and its failure to land marquee shows—the kind of shows that convince people to maintain their subscription instead of canceling it in favor of Disney+, Prime Video, or one of the many other options. While there’s little doubt that Netflix will make it through this challenge, it may never regain the market dominance it once held. I have a daring suggestion for Netflix that I’d like to think could help it: make more shows that are clean and suitable for family viewing. To frame it another way, stop putting sex, nudity, and harsh profanity in nearly every show. There are many potential subscribers who are eager to be entertained, but not by shows they deem unsuitable or inappropriate. I’ll grant that Netflix has viewing data that probably shows its TV-MA content is especially popular, but data can only count the people who watched a show, not the ones who never pressed play in the first place. It’s also can’t tell whether the sex and swearing are essential to those who watch it, or whether they’d watch it even if it was much cleaner. Perhaps you could think about it this way: a single explicit sex scene in a series is unlikely to gain more viewers than it will lose. What I mean is that a content warning on IMDb or Common Sense Media stating that a show contains an explicit sex scene is enough for me and many others to skip that entire show. But I very much doubt the opposite is true—that a person will learn that there is no sex scene and decide to skip it on that basis. Some of us simply have no interest in viewing such things and will guard both ourselves and our families from seeing them. For those who don’t believe me, I’m sure I can put them in touch with a thousand people who will affirm what I’m saying. I am convinced there is a deep desire for shows and movies we can watch without cringing, without feeling violated, and without knowing we are watching what we consider not only unsuitable but immoral. It’s not that we’re prudes or opposed to sex—we’re all for it. It’s that we think sex and nudity are for private contexts , not public ones, and that some matters are to be kept between a husband and wife rather than displayed for all the world to see. Sex is so good and powerful that making it public diminishes it rather than elevates it, which means we deliberately avoid shows and movies that feature it. I would say something similar about language . Does a show need to drop hundreds of F-bombs across just a few hours of runtime? Does it need to constantly use the name of God as a swear word? It doesn’t need any of this. Once again, is it more likely that people will not watch shows unless they are profane, or that they will avoid them if they are? I think the answer is obvious: many more people will be driven away by swearing than drawn by it. If I had to offer one more piece of advice, it would be to stop tampering with beloved intellectual properties to make them consistent with modern sensibilities. Why not just make adaptations that are faithful to the vision of the original creators and let us deal with the differences between their context and today’s? Have the courage to let the stories stand on their own without inserting modern messaging. I strongly believe this would do more to provoke good thinking and spur important conversations. There’s no need to be so heavy-handed. To summarize, I think Netflix has been going about this all wrong and has been creating content that alienates an eager audience rather than drawing it in. So Netfl",
   "content_zh": "Netflix似乎陷入了困难时期. 公司联合首席执行官最近宣布将解雇约1000名员工,这是多年来最大幅度的削减。 一天后,首席内容官突然退出,立即被替换. 仅仅一周后,市场对Netflix的Q3结果反应不佳,导致股票急剧下降.\n\nNetflix的悲哀有许多原因, 更是观众竞争日益激烈, 以及它未能登陆马奎节目,\n\nNetflix虽然毫无疑问会渡过这个挑战,但可能永远无法恢复它曾经占据的市场支配地位。 我对Netflix有一个大胆的建议, 我想可以帮助它: 做更多干净、适合家庭观看的节目。 以另一种方式设定它,停止把性,裸体,以及苛刻的淫秽在几乎所有的节目中.\n\n有许多潜在的订阅者渴望娱乐,但并不是通过他们认为不合适或不适当的展示。 我同意Netflix的收看数据可能显示其TV-MA内容特别受欢迎,\n\n也无法分辨性与誓词对于观看者是否至关重要, 也许你可以这样想: 系列中一个单一的显性场景不太可能获得比它失去的更多的观众.\n\n我的意思是,一个内容警告 互联网电影数据库(IMDb)上或Common Sense Media上 声明,一个节目包含一个明确的性场景,足够我和许多其他人跳过整个节目. 但我非常怀疑相反的情况是真实的——一个人会得知没有性场景,并据此决定跳过.\n\n我们中有些人根本不关心看这些东西,并将保护我们自己和我们的家人不看到它们。 我敢肯定, 我可以和一千人联系,\n\n我深信,人们非常渴望看一些节目和电影,而无需相信,无需感到被侵犯,也无需知道我们正在看我们认为不合适和不道德的东西。 我们不是故意或反对性爱,\n\n我们觉得性与裸体是私人事务, 性是如此的美好和强大, 使它公开 减少它而不是提升它, 这意味着我们故意避免 节目和电影 我会说类似的东西的语言。\n\n演出需要投下数百枚F炸弹吗? 难道它需要不断使用上帝之名作为誓词吗? 它不需要这些。 复次,是人不看秀,非有妄言,或避之不见,更何况?\n\n我认为答案是显而易见的:更多的人会因为宣誓而被驱逐,而不是被它吸引。 如果我不得不再提一个建议,那就是停止篡改所爱的知识产权,使之符合现代的敏感性.\n\n为何不只做忠实于原始创造者愿景的改编,让我们处理他们的背景与今天的不同? 要有勇气让这些故事独立存在,而不插入现代信息。 我坚信,这将更有助于激发良好的思维,刺激重要的对话。 也不必如此粗暴。\n\n总结一下,我认为Netflix一直把事情搞错了,并创造出让热心的观众疏远的内容,而不是把它引进来. 所以说网友",
   "image": "https://www.challies.com/media/2026/11/Midnight-Blue-Living-Room.png",
