  {
   "title": "How the Lord’s Supper Fuels Spiritual Growth",
   "url": "https://www.thegospelcoalition.org/article/lords-supper-spiritual-growth/",
   "category": "devotional",
   "source": "The Gospel Coalition",
   "source_url": "https://www.thegospelcoalition.org/feed/",
   "published": "2026-11-11",
   "summary": "Calvin shows us that the Lord’s Supper is fuel for spiritual growth, so we should come to the table expectantly every time.",
   "summary_zh": "加尔文向我们表明，主的晚餐是灵性成长的燃料，所以我们每次都应该期待地来到餐桌前。",
   "title_zh": "主的晚餐如何促进灵性成长",
   "content": "Recently, I (and my peers) have experienced a number of parishioners converting to Catholicism. As I’ve talked to these converts, they’ve consistently brought up the Lord’s Table. They find encouragement in identifying the bread and wine with Jesus’s body and blood. I’ve heard statements such as this: Pastor George, I like the idea that Jesus was talking about the Lord’s supper when he said, “Whoever feeds on my flesh and drinks my blood abides in me, and I in him” (John 6:56). My faith just feels more real if I’m literally bringing Jesus into my body on a daily basis. While this phenomenon seems to be happening with greater frequency, it’s not totally new. Years ago, I served alongside a man at an ordination service for another pastor in our city. I hadn’t met this man before; I only knew he had until recently been a professor at a Reformed and evangelical seminary. This man preached a solid message on Jesus’s call for ministers of the gospel, and I thanked him for it. Then, the next day, I saw him being interviewed on TV. I tuned in just as he explained, I needed more realism than my tradition provided, so during lunch I would walk down to the local Roman Catholic parish and take the Eucharist. To unite literally with Jesus’s body and blood was more deeply satisfying to me than the “mystery of union with Christ” my church taught. So, I resigned my post at the seminary and went home to Rome. I could relate other conversations with those who have left the Reformed faith and joined the Roman Catholic, Orthodox, or Anglo-Catholic traditions. A similar theme runs through them all: the desire for a more transformational, sensory reality than what the Reformed view of the Lord’s supper provides. Is this critique valid? How would John Calvin respond to the critique that if you follow the Reformed view of the supper, you’re missing out on its power? Transformed at the Table Sometimes, in an effort to have biblically faithful theology, Reformed Christians speak only of what the communion elements are not . I’m painfully aware of my of shortcomings in this regard. This can inadvertently drive some believers to seek out a more “real” experience in other traditions. We can get caught up in debating the exact nature of the bread and wine and forget to ask the more pressing question: How should this meal change me today? Let’s look back at Calvin’s teachings on communion. When we do, we’ll recover a renewed vision for the table, one that sees it as a divine encounter that motivates a transformed life of profound gratitude and obedience. In short, Calvin shows us that the Lord’s supper is fuel for spiritual growth, so we should come to the table expectantly every time. In Calvin’s “ Short Treatise on the Lord’s Supper ,” he writes that the supper fuels the believer’s spiritual growth in at least three ways. 1. The supper assures us of our inheritance in Christ. Calvin teaches that Jesus uses his table to assure us of our inheritance. He doesn’t merely mean that the supper gives us a psychological boost. He means that assurance comes by means of a real union between Christ and the believer, and he means that our experience of this union is expedited by faithfully partaking in the covenant meal. Calvin shows us that the Lord’s supper is fuel for spiritual growth, so we should come to the table expectantly every time. In his commentary on Ephesians, Calvin writes , “Paul says that we are members of his [Christ’s] flesh and of his bones. Do we wonder then, that in the Lord’s Supper he holds out his body to be enjoyed by us, and to nourish us unto eternal life?” Calvin insists that by faith, Christians are made members of Jesus’s fleshly human body, so at communion, we experience all the benefits of spiritual union with him. Christ took on flesh so that in his ascended state, he might be a channel of righteousness to the believer, pouring into him all that Christ’s human life accomplished. As a result of Jesus’s incarnation and death, God the Fa",
   "content_zh": "最近,我(和我的同龄人)经历了一些皈依天主教的教区人士. 当我和这些皈依者交谈时, 他们发现鼓励用耶稣的身体和血液来识别面包和葡萄酒。\n\n我听到过这样的话:乔治牧师,我喜欢耶稣在谈论主的晚餐,他说:“谁吃我的肉喝我的血,谁在我体内,我就在他体内”(John 6:56)。 如果我每天把耶稣带入我的身体, 虽然这种现象似乎更频繁地发生,\n\n多年前,我和一个男人一起 为我们市的另一位牧师服务 我之前从未见过这个人;我只知道他直到最近才成为一所改革后福音神学院的教授。 这个人在耶稣号召福音的大臣们时宣扬一个坚实的信息,我为此感谢他. 然后,第二天,我看到他接受电视采访。\n\n正如他所解释的,我需要比传统更现实的, 所以在午餐期间,我会走进罗马天主教当地的教区, 并带上尤查里会。 比起教会教义的“与基督结合的谜团”, 于是我辞去神学院的职务,回到了罗马.\n\n我可以和那些脱离改革后信仰并加入罗马天主教、东正教或天主教传统的人交谈。 一个类似的主题贯穿于所有这些方面:渴望一个比改革后对主的晚餐提供的看法更具有转型性、感官性的现实。 这种批评有道理吗?\n\n约翰·卡尔文会如何回应这种批评:如果你遵循改革后的晚餐观, 有时候,为了实现圣经上忠实的神学,改革后的基督徒只谈论圣餐的内容。 我痛苦地意识到我在这方面的缺点。\n\n这可能无意中驱使一些信徒在其他传统中寻求更“真实”的经验。 我们可以在辩论面包和葡萄酒的确切性质时陷入困境,并忘记提出更紧迫的问题: 今天这顿饭怎么能改变我? 让我们回顾加尔文关于圣餐的教义。\n\n我们将会重新恢复对桌子的憧憬, 简言之,加尔文告诉我们,主的晚餐是精神成长的燃料,因此,我们每次都应该期待地来到餐桌旁。\n\n他写道, 晚餐至少以三种方式促进信徒的精神成长。 1. 晚餐保证了我们在基督的遗产。 加尔文教耶稣用桌子保证我们的遗产 他不只是说晚餐会给我们心理上的刺激。\n\n他的意思是,通过基督和信徒之间的真正结合来保证,他的意思是,通过忠实地分享契约之餐来加快我们这一结合的经验。 加尔文向我们展示,主的晚餐是精神成长的燃料,因此,我们应当期待在每一次的餐桌上就餐。\n\nCalvin在其对以弗所发表的评论中写道:“保罗说,我们是他[基督]肉体和骨头的成员。\n\n难道我们不知道,在主的晚餐中,他伸出他的身体,让我们享受,并使我们获得永生吗? 加尔文坚持认为,根据信仰,基督徒是耶稣肉体上的身体的成员,因此在圣餐时,我们体验到与他精神结合的所有好处.\n\n基督将肉体取而代之,这样,在他升起的状态下,他可能成为信仰者的正义之道,将基督人类生命所成就的一切注入他体内. 由于耶稣的化身和死亡,上帝",
   "image": "https://media.thegospelcoalition.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/28193058/lords-supper-spiritual-growth.jpg",
   "author": "George Robertson",
