   "title": "I Regret to Inform You That Heaven Tourism Is Back",
   "url": "https://www.challies.com/book-reviews/i-regret-to-inform-you-that-heaven-tourism-is-back/",
   "category": "giant",
   "source": "Tim Challies",
   "source_url": "https://www.challies.com/feed/",
   "published": "2026-11-11",
   "summary": "I regret to inform you, dear reader, that heaven tourism is back. The genre possibly never went away entirely, but it did at least disappear from the bestseller lists for several years. Sadly, it has returned in Gabe Poirot’s 18 Days in Heaven. See Also My Top Book Reviews of All-Time The Bestsellers: Heaven Is For Real Greetings From Heaven: A Modern History of Heaven Tourism",
   "summary_zh": "亲爱的读者，很遗憾地通知您，天堂旅游又回来了。这种风格可能永远不会完全消失，但它至少在畅销书榜上消失了好几年。可悲的是，它在加布·波洛的《天堂18天》中又回来了。另请参阅我对畅销书的热门书评：天堂是真正的来自天堂的问候：天堂旅游的现代历史",
   "title_zh": "很遗憾地通知您，天堂旅游又回来了",
   "content": "I regret to inform you, dear reader, that heaven tourism is back. The genre possibly never went away entirely, but it did at least disappear from the bestseller lists for several years. Sadly, it has returned in Gabe Poirot’s 18 Days in Heaven , which was one of the bestselling Christian books last month and even made its way to the New York Times list of bestsellers—a feat most authors can only dream of. Out of sheer magnanimity (and perhaps a morbid sense of curiosity), I read it so you don’t have to. 18 Days in Heaven Gabe Poirot Before I tell you what it’s all about, here’s a brief history of heaven tourism. Don Piper kicked off the genre in 2004 with 90 Minutes in Heaven , and it proved a surprise hit that quickly dominated the sales charts, eventually tallying upwards of 8 million copies sold. Todd Burpo’s Heaven Is for Real followed in 2010 with over 10 million copies sold. That same year brought Kevin and Alex Malarkey’s The Boy Who Came Back from Heaven , which sold more than a million copies before Alex recanted his story and the publisher pulled it. The genre went on to deliver many more heaven books and a handful of less-popular hell books like Bill Wiese’s 23 Minutes in Hell , though none ever reached the same heights as the first few—no pun intended. My contribution to the genre was little more than reviewing several of the books and coining the snarky phrase “heaven tourism,” which, much to my delight, soon caught on . The genre inevitably ran its course, but has now returned in Gabe Poirot’s 18 Days in Heaven: I Left My Body. I Met Jesus. What He Told Me Will Alter Your Eternity . Sadly, but not surprisingly, it is just as bad as its predecessors, mixing truth with errors and documented facts with spurious speculations until it is as confusing and unreliable as any of them. 18 Days in Heaven follows the standard pattern by beginning with an account of a terrible accident—in this case, a head injury that resulted from a skateboarding mishap. Poirot, a young evangelist with a sizable YouTube following, was rendered comatose and remained hovering between life and death for 18 days. But while his body was inert, his soul was transported to heaven, where it experienced heavenly wonders, not the least of which was spending time with Jesus. What did he see and experience there? Many things, of course, some of which he is able to describe and many of which he insists he cannot, either because God told him not to or because they defy description. What can he describe? Jesus’s eyes, in which “I saw all the colors that had ever existed or could exist, all in one. He was the deepest ocean of liquid love that had no end. A spectrum of intensity that only increased the longer I stared. His eyebrows, hair, jawline, arms, chest, heart, stature, ears, forehead, eyelashes, teeth, mouth, skin, and complexion all said the same thing: I love you, I love you, I love you.” He describes Jesus’s scars which “were like an ocean that had no floor,” and had the name “Gabriel” written immediately above them. He learned from them that Jesus has completely and eternally forgotten his sins so that he could no longer remember them even if he wanted to. He smelled Jesus and observed that his “fragrance wasn’t like the humidity of the Florida Everglades. No, this was something eternal—an everlasting fountain of fire, not just surrounding me but burning into me. Wrapping around me like a robe, like a living garment of pure, unending presence. It felt burning hot and freezing cold at the same time.” He heard Jesus say many words and noted that his “words were not just a separate entity that He ‘released.’ They were like the sharpest spears that went right into the core of my very being. But these spears were not separate from Him—they were Him.” Poirot even saw his own conception: “The light from Father God united with the earthly seed from my father and the egg of my mother in that moment, and I began my existence on earth. I was not born just on",
   "content_zh": "我很遗憾地告诉你,亲爱的读者 天堂旅游又回来了 流派可能从未完全消失,但至少从畅销书榜上消失了几年.\n\n不幸的是,它回到了Gabe Poirot的\"天堂18天\"中,这是上个月最畅销的基督教书籍之一,甚至走进了纽约时报畅销书榜,这是大多数作者只能梦想的功绩。 我读了这篇文章,\n\n同年,凯文和亚历克丝·马拉克的\"从天堂回来的男孩\"(The Boy Who Came from Heaven)也带来了,后者在亚历克丝收回他的故事并被出版商拉出之前就售出超过一百万份. 虽然没有人能达到与前几本书相同的高度,\n\n我对这个流派的贡献不过是回顾几本书, 但现在又回到了Gabe Poirot在天堂的18天:我离开了我的身体。 我遇见了耶稣。 他告诉我 Will 改变你的永恒\n\n可悲但并不令人惊讶的是,它同其前身一样糟糕,把事实与错误相混淆,把有记录的事实同虚假的猜测相混淆,直到它同其中任何一个都一样令人困惑和不可靠。 18天的\"天堂\"遵循了标准模式,首先讲述了一起可怕的事故——在此情况下,由于滑板出事而造成头部受伤.\n\nPoirot是一位年轻的福音传播者,他带着一个庞大的YouTube跟随,因此被昏迷并徘徊在生与死之间长达18天. 但虽然他的身体是惰性的,他的灵魂被运到了天堂,在那里它经历了天堂的奇事,其中最起碼是和耶稣一起度过. 他在那里看到了什么? 体验什么?\n\n当然,许多事情,有些是他能够描述的,有些是他坚持不能描述的,要么是因为上帝告诉他不要描述,要么是因为他们藐视描述。 他能描述什么? 耶稣的眼睛,其中,“我看到了曾经存在或可能存在的所有颜色,都是一种颜色。 他是无尽的液体爱最深的海洋\n\n强度的光谱 只会增加我恒星的长度 他的眉毛、发型、下巴、手臂、胸部、心、身高、耳朵、额头、睫毛、牙齿、口腔、皮肤和相貌都说了同样的话:我爱你,我爱你,我爱你。” 他描述了耶稣的伤疤,这些伤疤 \" 像一片没有地的海洋 \" ,上面写有 \" Gabriel \" 的名字。\n\n他从他们那里得知,耶稣已经彻底地,永远地忘记了自己的罪过,这样即使他愿意也再也记不住了. 他闻到耶稣的气味并观察到他的\"香气不像佛罗里达埃弗格拉德河的湿润. 不,这是永恒的东西, 一个永恒的火焰喷泉, 不仅包围着我,而且燃烧在我身上。\n\n缠绕我就像长袍一样 像纯洁无尽的活衣 它同时感到被烧得发热而冷冻。” 他听到耶稣说出许多话,并指出他的\"词不仅是一个他‘释放'的独立实体. 他们就像最锋利的长矛 直入我生命的核心\n\n这些长矛,不是与他分开的,他们确是他。 波洛甚至看到了自己的构想:“神父所赐的光与我父亲所赐的地上种子和母亲的卵合而为一,我开始了我在地上的存在。 我不是刚出生",
   "image": "https://www.challies.com/media/2026/11/Vintage-Suitcase-and-Open-Books.png",
   "author": "Tim Challies",
   "tags": [
    "Book Reviews"
   ],
   "language": "en",
   "country": "CA",
   "region": "",
   "stance": "evangelical",
   "flags": [],
   "priority": 2,
   "fetched_at": "2026-11-11T00:26:27.436583",
   "extra": {
    "feed_type": "rss20"
   },
   "id": "34c370d646be8ed1"
  },
  {
   "title": "A La Carte (November 6)",
   "url": "https://www.challies.com/a-la-carte/a-la-carte-november-6-2026/",
   "category": "giant",
   "source": "Tim Challies",
   "source_url": "https://www.challies.com/feed/",
   "published": "2026-11-11",
   "summary": "Don’t despise the red flags / Your thoughts are shaping your life / Can’t you just love everyone? / Selfies are not the self: forming embodied youth in a disembodied age / Effective altruists and the spreadsheet gospel / 4 Applications for the order of love—from the home to the ends of the earth / Kindle deals, and more See Also Weekend A La Carte (March 1) A La Carte (February 3) A La Carte (September 23)",
   "summary_zh": "不要轻视危险信号/你的思想正在塑造你的生活/你不能只爱每个人吗？/Selfies are not the self: forming embodied youth in a unembodied age/Effective altruists and the spreadsheet gospel/4申请爱的顺序-从家到地球的尽头/Kindle优惠等另请参阅周末点菜（ 3月1日）点菜（ 2月3日）点菜（ 9月23日）",
   "title_zh": "点菜（ 11月6日）",
   "content": "Good morning. I’ve got A La Carte and a regretful book review for you today. Over the weekend you’ll find Saturday’s Weekend A La Carte plus Sunday’s Works & Wonders, which shares content that is just right for a Sunday morning. I hope you enjoy reading it all as much as I have enjoyed putting it together. Sales & Deals Today’s Kindle deals are headlined by Joel Beeke’s A Radical, Comprehensive Call to Holiness . You may also be interested in a book about women who overcame a difficult past or a biography written for a younger audience. Recommended Reading Selfies Are Not the Self: Forming Embodied Youth in a Disembodied Age. This article from Harvest USA has some helpful insights about young people and their mobile devices. “Increasingly, research shows that teenagers know their technology is making them miserable. They don’t love their phones, but they hate missing out. They know the noise is making them sick, but they’re terrified of the silence. When teenagers are so thirsty for connection and validation that they’ll go to a well they know is poisoned just to get a drink, what an amazing opportunity we have as the church to offer something better, something truly beautiful. We don’t want our youth ministries to be known for what they lack. We want them to be known for their abundance.” Can’t You Just Love Everyone? Jeremy Walker addresses churches that think their calling is to “just love everyone.” “Why not just love one another? Why build walls, make distinctions, establish boundaries, and draw lines? Why confront sins? Why not welcome all equally? Can’t you just love everyone?” 4 Applications for the Order of Love—From the Home to the Ends of the Earth. Speaking of love, Alex Kocman addresses the necessity of rightly ordering our love. “Augustine’s idea of ordo amoris —the ‘order of love’—reflects the Scriptural pattern of directing our affections to first love God, then to love our families, and then to extend that love outward to others. This theme of ordered love has wide-ranging implications—from the home to the ends of the earth. But just as preaching without application is lifeless, so too is a study of love without practical exhortation. Here are four admonitions…” Effective Altruists and the Spreadsheet Gospel. Writing for TGC, Nathan Leamer addresses the effective altruism ideology that is so influential in Silicon Valley. “Over the past decade, an emergent secular philosophy has taken root in Silicon Valley. It now drives many conversations around politics and technology, particularly as its ideas shape how our society approaches artificial intelligence. Known as ‘effective altruism,’ this worldview is having a moment. The Economist recently called the philosophy ‘this century’s biggest idea’ and labeled the movement as ‘admirable, but dangerous.'” Your Thoughts Are Shaping Your Life. “Your thoughts are shaping your life.” That might sound like the kind of thing Joel Osteen or a New Age practitioner would say, but there is truth to it. “Our thought life matters more than we often realize. The conversations we replay in our minds, the offenses we revisit, and the fears we entertain can shape how we see our circumstances and respond to the people around us. I know how easily my thoughts can become consumed by something that has happened or something I fear might happen. Before long, I find myself responding to the present through the lens of thoughts I have been rehearsing.” Don’t Despise the Red Flags. Katie Laitkep writes to those who are dating, and to young women specifically, to warn them not to ignore or despise the red flags. “When we see signs marking a dangerous road, we respond immediately, knowing we need to find a different path. But when it comes to red flags in dating relationships, we don’t always view the warning signs as clearly.” Reviews I like to share the occasional reminder that it is tremendously helpful to authors if you post even brief reviews of their books at Amazon. On that note, it wo",
   "content_zh": "早上好,你好吗? 今天我为你们准备了一本《La Carte》, 在周末, 你会发现周六的周末A La Carte 加上周日的作品与奇事, 我希望你喜欢阅读这一切 就像我喜欢把它拼凑在一起。\n\n今天的销售和交易由Joel Beeke的激进、全面呼吁宗座作标题。 你也许也对一本关于克服了困难的过去的妇女的书或一本为年轻观众所写的传记感兴趣。 推荐阅读的自我不是自我:在身体衰弱的时代培养健康青年.\n\n“收获美国”的这篇文章对年轻人及其移动设备有一些有益的见解。 “令人不安的是,研究表明,青少年知道他们的技术正在使他们痛苦地生活。 他们不喜欢手机, 但他们对沉默感到恐惧。\n\n当青少年渴求联系和验证, 他们就会去井里, 我们不希望我们的青年部因缺乏什么而出名。 我们要使他们富足而闻名。” 你不能只爱每个人吗?\n\n杰里米,你好吗? Walker)在教会发表演说, “为什么不只是彼此相爱呢? 为何要建起围墙,作出区分,确定界限,并划定界限? 为什么要面对罪恶? 为什么不平等地欢迎所有人? 你不能只爱每一个人吗?”\n\n说到爱情,亚历克丝·克曼谈到 正确订购我们的爱的必要性。 \" 奥古斯丁对ordo amoris的想法——`爱的顺序'——反映了将我们的感情引导到首先爱上帝,然后爱我们的家人,然后将这种爱向外延伸的文字模式。 这个有秩序的爱情主题有着从家到地底的广泛影响.\n\n但是,正如没有应用的传道一样,没有实际劝告的爱的研究也是没有生命的. 以下是四点忠告...\"有效的 Altruists和\"电子表格福音\". 为TGC写作,内森·莱默(Nathan Leamer)谈到了在硅谷有如此影响力的有效利他主义意识形态. “过去十年来,一种新兴的世俗哲学在硅谷扎根。\n\n它现在推动了许多关于政治和技术的对话,特别是它的思想决定了我们社会如何对待人工智能。 这个世界观被称为“有效的利他主义”, 经济学人最近称这个哲学为\"本世纪最大的思想\"并称这个运动为\"可喜而危险\". 你的思想正在塑造你的人生\n\n“你的思想正在塑造你的生活。” 听起来像乔尔·奥斯丁 或者新时代的实践者会说的 但事实确实如此 “我们的思想生活比我们经常意识到的更重要。 我们在思想中重现的谈话,我们重温的罪恶,以及我们所娱乐的恐惧,可以塑造我们如何看待我们的情况并回应周围的人.\n\n我知道我的思想很容易被 已经发生的事情或我担心的事情所消耗 在不久之前,我发现自己通过我所听到的思维的镜头对现在作出反应。” 不要让红旗队失望。 凯蒂·莱特克普(Katie Laitkep)给那些正在约会的人写信,并特别给年轻妇女写信,警告她们不要忽视或轻视红旗.\n\n“当我们看到标志一条危险道路的迹象时,我们立即作出反应,知道我们需要找到另一条道路。 但对于恋爱关系中的红旗, 作者若在亚马逊发表简短的评论, 如此一来,它就",
