  {
   "title": "Build a Culture of Evangelism",
   "url": "https://www.thegospelcoalition.org/podcasts/everyday-pastor/build-culture-evangelism/",
   "category": "devotional",
   "source": "The Gospel Coalition",
   "source_url": "https://www.thegospelcoalition.org/feed/",
   "published": "2026-11-11",
   "summary": "Matt Smethurst and Ligon Duncan discuss how pastors can build a culture of evangelism by modeling gospel conversations, equipping church members to share their faith, and making evangelism a natural part of church life.",
   "summary_zh": "马特·斯梅瑟斯特（ Matt Smethurst ）和利贡·邓肯（ Ligon Duncan ）讨论了牧师如何通过模仿福音对话，装备教会成员分享他们的信仰，并使传福音成为教会生活的自然组成部分来建立传福音的文化。",
   "title_zh": "建立传福音的文化",
   "content": "How can pastors help their churches become more evangelistic without relying on another program? Matt Smethurst and Ligon Duncan discuss what it takes to build a culture where sharing the gospel becomes a normal part of church life. Resources Mentioned: Before You Share Your Faith by Matt Smethurst Evangelism by J. Mack Stiles The Gospel and Personal Evangelism by Mark Dever Out of the Saltshaker and into the World by Rebecca Manley Pippert The Art of Asking Better Questions by J. R. Briggs If you’re ready to go deeper, Southern Seminary’s PhD program is where that begins. Visit sbts.edu/phd to learn more.",
   "content_zh": "牧师怎么能不依赖另一个方案,就帮助他们的教会变得更加福音派? 马特·斯梅特赫斯特(Matt Smethurst)和利贡·邓肯(Ligon Duncan)讨论建立一种共享福音成为教会生活中一个正常部分的文化需要什么. 资源参考:在你分享你的信仰之前,由Matt Smethurst Evangelism由J.\n\nMack Stiles The Glass and Personal Evangelism由马克·德弗出自\"盐沙克\"并进入了世界,由丽贝卡·曼利·皮珀特(英语:Rebecca Manley Pippert)出自J. R. Briggs所著\"问更好的问题的艺术\". 南方神学院的博士课程就是开始的地方。 访问 sbts.edu/phd 学习更多.",
   "image": "https://media.thegospelcoalition.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/30144332/The-Everyday-Pastor-Thumbnail-16x9-%E2%80%93-59.jpg",
   "author": "Matt Smethurst, Ligon Duncan",
   "tags": [
    "Podcast",
    "Evangelism"
   ],
   "language": "en",
   "country": "US",
   "region": "",
   "stance": "evangelical",
   "flags": [],
   "priority": 2,
   "fetched_at": "2026-11-11T00:25:53.147536",
   "extra": {
    "feed_type": "rss20"
   },
   "id": "f5f1b2cf1f023e09"
  },
  {
   "title": "Train Women to Teach the Bible: A 4-Week Plan",
   "url": "https://www.thegospelcoalition.org/article/train-women-teach-bible/",
   "category": "devotional",
   "source": "The Gospel Coalition",
   "source_url": "https://www.thegospelcoalition.org/feed/",
   "published": "2026-11-11",
   "summary": "Bible studies are the heartbeat of women’s ministries in many churches. But where do we find teachers?",
   "summary_zh": "圣经学习是许多教会中妇女事工的心跳。但是我们在哪里可以找到老师？",
   "title_zh": "培训女性教导圣经： 4周计划",
   "content": "Bible studies are the heartbeat of women’s ministries in many churches. More and more churches have come to prefer live teaching over video studies . But this can present a challenge for women’s ministry directors: Where do we find teachers? Bible teachers don’t come ready-made. We have to raise them up through training. Whether women have never taught the Scriptures before or are building on previous experience, we can help them grow and gain confidence in “rightly handling the word of truth” (2 Tim. 2:15) through careful instruction and practice. To equip women in my church to teach, I helped develop a four-session course. Here’s how we developed and implemented this training. Get Organized I first recruited two women with experience teaching the Bible, and together we prepared ourselves as instructors by taking the online Simeon Trust First Principles for Women course. We then personalized the principles we learned to fit our training goals for other women in our church. Since there are different strategies for approaching various genres of Scripture, the first time we held this course, we chose to train women on how to teach an epistle. We selected 1 Peter as our text and assigned each group a passage to work on throughout the course. For example, Group 1 had 1 Peter 1:1–12, Group 2 had 1:13–21, and Group 3 had 4:12–19. We used 5:1–11 as a common passage that we studied as a whole group. In subsequent courses, we chose other genres of Scripture: narrative using passages from a Gospel and wisdom/poetry using a psalm. Bible teachers don’t come ready-made. We have to raise them up through training. We promoted the training with a clear purpose: to help participants grow in their ability to understand and teach God’s Word. We spelled out our expectations: attending all four sessions, completing homework, and teaching a 10-minute message to peers. Individual attention in training is key, so we assigned each participant to a small group of three to six women with a leader. The leader gave feedback on homework and answered questions. Teach the Course Here’s what we covered each week, including teaching content, small-group work, and homework assignments. Week 1: Foundations of Study After sharing two foundational truths about the Bible—Scripture is God-breathed (2 Tim. 3:16–17) and Scripture is alive and active, changing us as we study it (Heb. 4:12)—we taught three principles. First, we never add to or subtract from what’s written in the Word (Deut. 4:2; 1 Cor. 4:6; Rev. 22:18–19). A Bible text isn’t a jumping-off point to give our insights. We must be diligent to stay faithful to the Holy Spirit’s intended meaning. We strive to “stay on the line” of Scripture , never going above it by adding to it (which can result in undue speculation or legalism) or going below it by taking away from it (avoiding unpleasant or confusing passages, or altering the meaning to what we want it to mean). Second, the Bible is one big story, with one Hero of the story (Luke 24:27). From Genesis to Revelation—creation, fall, redemption, and restoration—we see God at work to reconcile people to himself. The Old Testament points to our need for and anticipation of a Savior, and the New Testament records and reflects on that Savior. Understanding this one big story helps reveal the significance of each passage. Third, context is king and questions are key . Context is the setting in which something occurs, and it always guides our interpretation. We consider both the literary context (what comes before and after a passage) and the historical context (the situation in which the original audience found themselves). To “stay on the line,” asking in-depth questions of the passage is vital, so we introduced the participants to questions for an epistle . In small-group time, we had each woman read the group’s assigned passage aloud because a thoughtful reading aids understanding, as does hearing the passage multiple times. For homework, we asked the women to l",
   "content_zh": "圣经研究是许多教会中妇女各部的心跳。 越来越多的教会更喜欢现场教学, 这可能会给妇女部主任带来挑战: 我们在哪里找到老师? 圣经老师不会来。 我们必须通过训练培养他们。\n\n无论妇女以前或现在都从未教授过《圣经》,我们都能通过认真的教导和实践,帮助她们成长并获得“正确处理真相”的信心(2 Tim.2:15)。 为了让教会里的女人能够教书, 我帮助开发了一个四会的课程。 以下是我们如何发展和实施这种培训。\n\nGet Organization I首先招募了两位有教授\"圣经\"经验的女性,我们一起通过上线的\"西门信托第一妇女原则\"课程,准备成为教官. 然后,我们将我们学到的原则个性化,以适应我们为教会中其他妇女的培训目标。\n\n由于有不同的策略 接近各种流派的经典, 我们第一次举办这个课程, 我们选择培训妇女 如何教授一个故事。 我们挑选了一名彼得作为我们的案文,并指派每个小组在全程中工作一段。 例如,第1组有1个Peter 1:1–12,第2组有1:13–21,第3组有4:12–19.\n\n我们用5:1–11作为我们作为一个整体研究的共同段落. 在随后的课程中,我们选择了其他的\"圣经\"流派:用福音书的段落进行叙事,而用诗作\"智慧\"/\"诗\". 圣经老师不会来。 我们必须通过训练培养他们。\n\n我们以明确的目的促进了培训:帮助参与者提高理解和教授上帝之道的能力。 我们阐述了我们的期望:出席所有四场会议,完成作业,向同行传授十分钟的信息。 对培训的个别关注是关键所在,因此,我们指派每个参与者到一个由3至6名妇女组成的小组,并有一名领导人。\n\n4:12)——我们教授了三项原则. 首先,我们从不增减《言语》中的内容(德国:4:2;1 Cor:4:6;Rev:22:18-19)。 圣经的文本不是给我们深刻见解的跳出点。 我们必须勤奋地忠于圣灵的本意。\n\n我们努力“坚持”《圣经》,决不通过增加《圣经》而超越《圣经》(这可能导致不适当的猜测或法律主义),或通过取走《圣经》而低于《圣经》(避免令人不快或令人困惑的段落,或改变《圣经》的含义)。 第二,\"圣经\"是一个大故事,有一个故事的英雄(公爵24:27).\n\n从创世纪到启示——创造、倒下、赎回、复原——我们看见上帝在努力使人民与自己和解。 \"旧约\"指出我们对救世主的需求和期待,\"新约\"记载并反思了\"救世主\". 了解这个大故事有助于揭示每个段落的意义. 第三,背景是国王,问题是关键。\n\n环境是事物发生的环境,它总是指导我们的解释。 我们既考虑文学背景(一段前后发生的事),也考虑历史背景(原始观众发现自己的处境). 因此,我们向与会者介绍了一些问题。\n\n我们让每个女人大声读到他们指定的段落, 做功课时,我们要求女人",
