  {
   "title": "The Flavors of Holy Joy",
   "url": "https://rss.desiringgod.org/link/10732/17518289/the-flavors-of-holy-joy",
   "category": "devotional",
   "source": "Desiring God",
   "source_url": "https://www.desiringgod.org/blog.rss",
   "published": "2026-11-11",
   "summary": "A well-cooked meal and a newborn baby fill our hearts with different delights. Pastor John explains how God’s gifts offer us a multicolored experience of joy. Listen Now",
   "summary_zh": "一顿精心烹饪的饭菜和一个刚出生的婴儿让我们心中充满了不同的乐趣。约翰牧师解释了神的恩赐如何给我们带来多彩的喜乐体验。现在聆听",
   "title_zh": "神圣欢乐的味道",
   "content": "Audio Transcript We like to think in simple categories. It’s easier that way, and easier to be clear in explaining things. So, if I ask, “How are you doing?” you will probably respond in terms of “good” or “bad,” and then explain why you are feeling good or bad. By default, we are doing fine until we are doing bad, but it’s normally one or the other, up or down like a light switch: We’re good. We’re bad. Then along comes the apostle Paul and pulls this on-off switch off the wall and breaks it into pieces to teach us that the very good and the very bad coexist in this life. Today on Ask Pastor John: the many flavors of holy joy. It’s a question from an anonymous listener to the podcast. “Pastor John, you have been writing and speaking for decades about the importance of joy in the life of God and the life of a Christian. You have made the case that joy in God — not only in his gifts but in his person — is essential to glorifying God. You have argued that the reason that God’s God-centeredness, his pursuit of his own glorification in all he does, is not megalomania is precisely because he magnifies his glory through our enjoyment of him. You have pointed out that the apostle Paul not only spoke of being ‘sorrowful, yet always rejoicing,’ but that he also said, ‘In all our affliction, I am overflowing with joy’ (2 Corinthians 6:10; 7:4). That last statement seems staggering. If it can be present in such different circumstances, can you help me even begin to imagine what true joy is?” Joy in All Circumstances That very statement from 2 Corinthians 7:4 has arrested my attention for a little over a year (since I taught 2 Corinthians last year) and just wouldn’t let me go, because it seems to demand that we dig into the nature of joy to figure out what he could possibly mean by calling his experience joyful in the light of so many different sufferings. He mentions 26 sufferings, different kinds of affliction in 2 Corinthians 11:23–28. It’s not just Paul’s experience of joy in all those afflictions that drives me, but many, many different circumstances in the Bible are the circumstances in which we are told to rejoice. It’s just incredible how many there are. Here are a few. - Rejoice in your future inheritance (1 Peter 1:3–6). - Rejoice that your names are written in heaven (Luke 10:20). - Rejoice in the plundering of your property for love’s sake (Hebrews 10:34). - Rejoice in being poured out for others (Philippians 2:17–18). - Rejoice in spending and being spent for souls (2 Corinthians 12:15). - Rejoice in your weakness for Christ’s sake (2 Corinthians 12:9–10). - Rejoice in being slandered and persecuted and rejected (Matthew 5:11–12; Luke 6:22–23). - Rejoice in being shamed for Christ’s name (Acts 5:41). - Rejoice in contributing to others’ needs (2 Corinthians 9:7). - Rejoice in the partnership of other believers (Philippians 1:4–5). - Rejoice in people being converted (1 Thessalonians 2:19–20). - Rejoice in the unity of believers (Philippians 2:2). - Rejoice in truth (1 Corinthians 13:6). - Rejoice in the law of God (Psalm 119:14, 111, 162). - Rejoice in the Lord (Philippians 4:4). - Rejoice in the hope of the glory of God (Romans 5:2). - Rejoice in God (Romans 5:11). That’s for starters. The variety of circumstances in which we are told to rejoice demands this: Joy is not a static emotion but one that changes in intensity. That’s pretty obvious for everybody, but also — this is what I’m drawing out now that feels fresh to me — it varies in quality. So, what do I mean by quality? I mean the nature or the kind of experience joy is — not just how intense it is but how different it is, or how it can feel so different from one kind of experience to the next. And yet, all of them could be called joy, which I think means a positive emotion that we really want to have. That would be my kind of bottom-line definition of joy. Six Qualities of Joy For example, I think we can distinguish (this is my best effort so far; I’m pressing into",
   "content_zh": "音频记录 我们喜欢简单思考 这样比较容易, 因此,如果我问到“你在做什么?” 你可能会回答“好”或“坏”,然后解释你为什么感觉好或坏。 默认情况下,我们做得很好,直到我们做坏事为止,但通常它是一个或另一个,上或下像一个开关:我们很好。\n\n我们是坏人。 然后使徒保罗来了 把这个关上 然后把它拆成碎片 教我们 生活中的善恶共存 今天在\"问约翰牧师\"(Ask Pastor John):许多圣乐的口味. 这是匿名听众对播客的提问。\n\n“约翰神父,几十年来你一直在写作和谈论欢乐在上帝生活中的重要性和基督徒的生活。 你已说明,上帝的喜悦——不仅是他的礼物,而且是他个人的喜悦——对颂扬上帝至关重要。\n\n你们认为,上帝的以神为本,他追求自己的美德,并不是因为大浪漫通过我们享受他的光荣。\n\n你已指出使徒保罗不仅说出\"悲伤而总是欢喜\",他还说:\"在我们所有的苦难中,我充满了欢快\" (2 Corinthians 6: 10; 7: 4). 刚才的发言令人吃惊。\n\n如果在这样不同的情况下它能够存在,你能帮助我开始想象真正的快乐是什么吗?” 所有情况下的欢乐 2个哥林多人7: 4的言论让我的注意力 一年多一点(因为去年我教过2个哥林多人),\n\n在如此多不同痛苦的光芒下 称他的经历很快乐 他提到26起痛苦, 不仅仅是保罗在所有这些苦难中所经历的快乐, 这真是不可思议。\n\n这里有一些。 - 享受你未来的遗产(1 Peter 1: 3-6) - 很高兴你们的名字写在天上(公爵10:20) - 在为爱而掠夺你的财产(赫布鲁斯10:34)中欢欣鼓舞. - 欢呼为他人倒出(菲利浦2:17-18)。 - 享受为灵魂而花和花的快乐(2科林斯人12:15).\n\n- 为基督欢呼你的软弱(2科林斯人12:9-10). 被诽谤、迫害和拒绝(马修5:11–12;卢克6:22–23)。 - 乐于满足他人的需要(2科林斯9:7)。 - 与其他信徒合作欢呼(菲利浦1:4-5)。\n\n- 很高兴有人被改造(1 Thessalonians 2:19-20)。 - 欢呼信徒的团结(菲利浦2:2). - 真理快乐(1科林斯13:6). - 在\"上帝之法\"中取悦(\"诗经\"119:14,111,162). - 欢乐于主(菲利浦4:4). - 为上帝的荣耀而欢呼(罗马人5:2). - 享受上帝(罗马人5:11). 这首先要说明,\n\n我们被告知欢乐的环境多种多样,这就要求我们:欢乐不是一种静态的情感,而是一种强度变化的情感. 这对每个人都是显而易见的,但同样——这是我现在所画的,我觉得很新鲜——它的质量也各不相同。 那么,我是什么意思 质量?\n\n我指的是快乐的本质或体验类型——不仅仅是它有多强烈,而是它有多不同,或者它能有什么感觉,从一种体验到另一种体验如此不同. 然而,所有它们都可以叫做快乐, 我认为这意味着我们真正想要有一种积极的情感。 那就是我对快乐的底线定义\n\n例如,我想我们可以分辨一下(这是我迄今所做的最大努力;我迫切要求)",
   "image": "https://dg.imgix.net/resources/17932/media/7f07b0d2-74ba-4096-8793-a57fc3bf9aba/landscape/the-flavors-of-holy-joy-en.jpg?ts=1791815766&ixlib=rails-4.3.1&fit=crop&w=2000&h=1050",
