  },
  {
   "title": "Don’t Forget the Great Commission When You Counsel",
   "url": "https://www.9marks.org/article/dont-forget-the-great-commission-when-you-counsel/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=dont-forget-the-great-commission-when-you-counsel",
   "category": "devotional",
   "source": "9Marks",
   "source_url": "https://9marks.org/feed/",
   "published": "2026-11-11",
   "summary": "The Great Commission, then, is for fearful, doubting, weak, disbelieving disciples, who need to be reminded of Jesus’s power and authority.   The post  Don’t Forget the Great Commission When You Counsel  appeared first on  9Marks .",
   "summary_zh": "因此，大使命是针对恐惧、怀疑、软弱、不信的门徒，他们需要被提醒耶稣的能力和权柄。当你的律师首次出现在9Marks上时，不要忘记伟大的佣金。",
   "title_zh": "为您提供咨询服务时，不要忘记高额佣金",
   "content": "Abstract: Nick Gardner reminds pastors of the usefulness of the Great Commission when they counsel church members. Jesus gave the church the task of making disciples, which involves applying his teaching to the sin and suffering of his people. In the midst of trials, disciples of Jesus need to be reminded that their Lord and Savior has all authority in the universe and promises to be with them to the end of the age. John walked into my office again. His head was hanging low. He felt lonely and betrayed by his friends again. Susan was in my office talking about how her boss, a church member, was not easy to work with. David and Joanna were grieving as their wayward child was making poor choices. What do all of these have in common? These are Christians who are suffering and need help. They have been let down, they have been hurt, and they need the Great Commission. The Great Commission? That’s right. Pastor, when you sit across from someone who is suffering from their own sin, from the sin of someone else, or from living in a fallen world, don’t forget the Great Commission. In Matthew 28:18–20, we read, And Jesus came and said to them, “All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me. Go therefore and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, teaching them to observe all that I have commanded you. And behold, I am with you always, to the end of the age.” Remember that the disciples were not exactly brimming with confidence around the time that Jesus spoke these words to them. They had just been: Hiding behind locked doors out of fear of others harming them (John 20:19). Doubting the veracity of the resurrection as told by Mary Magdalene (Luke 24:10). “Startled and frightened and thought they saw a spirit” (Luke 24:37). Doubting the reality of Jesus right in front of them (Matt. 28:17; Luke 24:10–11). The Great Commission, then, is for fearful, doubting, weak, disbelieving disciples, who need to be reminded of Jesus’s power and authority. Here are four reasons to remember these parting words from Jesus as you counsel members of your church through suffering and sin. 1. Jesus Is Sovereign over Everything Echoing Daniel 7:13–14, Jesus makes the startling claim that the entire cosmos belongs to him and is subject to his governing power. This includes the suffering and sin of his people. And this sovereignty comes from his victory over sin and death, for he is the one who healed diseases, hurled out demons, humbled boastful men, and humiliated death. Jesus’s sovereignty is good news because of how he earned it: through his perfect obedience, substitutionary death, and triumphant resurrection. This is the truth you hold out to suffering saints: Christ is ruling and will subdue all their enemies. The authority of Jesus over heaven and earth also includes his authorization of you as a pastor to care for his sheep. As Harold Senkbeil notes, “We have no authorization to do ministry apart from our Lord Jesus. That means we care for the souls entrusted to us according to his express purpose and will.” Harold Senkbeil, The Care of Souls: How to Cultivate a Pastor’s Heart (Lexham Press, 2019), 98. But Jesus isn’t only sovereign over your life; he is sovereign over everything. Suffering has the power to shrink our world from being God’s grand theater of redemption to being more like our walk-in closet of personal suffering. The Great Commission helps us remember that our suffering is not ultimate or all-powerful, as it serves God’s greater purposes in the world. So, pastor, present this bigger picture of God’s supreme authority in your counseling. 2. The Goal of Biblical Counseling Is Making Disciples Biblical counseling is about growth and maturity as a disciple of Christ . Whether someone is struggling with a miscarriage or a besetting sin, the goal is for them to “remain steadfast under trial” (Jas. 1:12) in order that they may one day “see God” (Matt. 5:8). The",
   "content_zh": "摘要:尼克·加德纳(Nick Gardner)提醒牧师,当他们为教会成员提供咨询时,大委员会的用处. 耶稣赋予教会造门徒的任务,这涉及到将他的教导应用于他的人民的罪恶和苦难.\n\n在考验中,耶稣的门徒需要被提醒,他们的主和救世主在宇宙中拥有一切权威,并承诺与他们同在,直到时代结束. 约翰又走进我的办公室了 他的头被吊低了 他感到孤独,再次被朋友背叛. Susan在我的办公室里 谈到她的老板,一个教会成员, 如何不容易工作。\n\nDavid和Joanna伤心欲绝 因为他们走投无路的孩子 选择不好 所有这些有什么共同点? 他们是受苦受难的基督徒,需要帮助。 他们已经失望,他们受到伤害,他们需要大委员会。 大委员会? 没错。\n\n牧师,当你坐在一个人的对面, 谁在遭受自己的罪恶, 别人的罪恶, 或生活在一个堕落的世界, 在马太福音28:18-20中,我们读到了,耶稣来对他们说:\"天地间的一切权力,都授予了我.\n\n故去造万国弟子,以父子圣灵之名施洗,教其观察我所命之一切. 我将永远与你们同在,直到他们的寿终。 记住,耶稣对他们讲这些话的时候,门徒们并没有完全充满信心。\n\n因为害怕别人伤害他们(John 20:19), 怀疑玛丽·马格达伦(英语:Mary Magdalene)(Luke 24:10)所说的复活的真实性. “开始害怕,以为他们看到了一种精神”(公爵24:37)。 怀疑耶稣就在他们面前的现实(Matt. 28:17;Luke 24:10–11).\n\n因此,大委员会是为恐惧,怀疑,软弱,不信的门徒,他们需要被提醒耶稣的力量和权威. 这里有四个理由来纪念耶稣的这些离散之词,当你通过苦难和罪恶来劝告你的教会成员. 一、导 言\n\n耶稣是万物之王,回声丹尼尔7:13-14,耶稣发出惊人的宣称,整个宇宙属于他,并服从他的统治权. 这包括其人民的痛苦和罪恶。 而这个主权来自他战胜罪恶和死亡,因为他是治愈疾病,吐出恶魔,谦卑自夸的人,羞辱死亡的人.\n\n耶稣的主权是好消息,因为他是如何赢得主权的:通过他的完全服从、替代死亡和胜利的复活。 这就是你对受苦受难的圣人所持的真理: 基督正在统治,并将征服他们的敌人。 耶稣对天地的权威还包括他授权你作为牧师照顾他的羊.\n\n正如Harold Senkbeil指出的,“除了我们的主耶稣之外,我们无权从事任何事务。 这意味着我们关心根据他明确的宗旨和意愿赋予我们的灵魂。” Harold Senkbeil, The Care of Souls: How to 培植牧师的心(莱克汉姆出版社,2019),98. 但耶稣不仅对你的人生拥有主权,\n\n苦难可以使我们的世界从上帝的救赎之大剧院缩水, 大委员会帮助我们记住,我们的痛苦不是终极的,也不是万能的,因为它符合上帝在世界上更大的目的。 因此,牧师,在你的咨询中,呈现上帝至高无上的威信。 2. 国家\n\n《圣经咨询》的目标, 无论有人在为流产或制造罪恶而挣扎,他们都希望“在审判中坚持不懈”(Jas 1:12),以便有一天“见上帝”(Matt 5:8)。 这个",
