   "title": "A La Carte (November 10)",
   "url": "https://www.challies.com/a-la-carte/a-la-carte-november-10-2026/",
   "category": "giant",
   "source": "Tim Challies",
   "source_url": "https://www.challies.com/feed/",
   "published": "2026-11-11",
   "summary": "C.S. Lewis's vices / How to graciously say no / Can’t you let me just attend? / When morningness is confused with godliness / Everyone doesn’t react the same way / Evangelizing with a dog / Kindle deals / New books See Also A La Carte (1/12) A La Carte (11/10) A La Carte (12/2)",
   "summary_zh": "C.S. Lewis的恶习/如何慷慨地说不/你不能让我参加吗？/当早晨与虔诚混淆时/每个人的反应都不一样/与狗一起传福音/Kindle优惠/新书另请参阅单点菜肴（ 1/12 ）单点菜肴（ 11/10 ）单点菜肴（ 12/2 ）",
   "title_zh": "点菜（ 11月10日）",
   "content": "Let me take a moment to remind you of some of the features of this site you may not know about. I have a growing page of Liturgies meant to help the local church in its worship. I also offer Book Recommendations across many categories and a list of the Best Commentaries for each book of the Bible. And if you like the quote graphics I share day by day, you can find them all at SquareQuotes.church where they are free to download. Sales & Deals Today’s Kindle deals include several of the “Heart Talks” mini books that are meant to help women with common concerns: feeling like failures in parenting, husbands who lead poorly, doubt, and so on. You’ll also find Greg Gifford’s Lies My Therapist Told Me and several other titles. Recommended Reading Beer, Beef and Player’s Navy Cut. This is a brilliant article about C.S. Lewis and the vices that dogged him throughout his life—a three-pack-a day smoking habit, eating habits that bordered on gluttony, and far more alcohol than anyone should consume. “In case you really are new here and you mostly know Lewis from the kid’s books, let’s state the obvious: the man we universally honor as one of the 20th Century’s great saints smoked, ate and drank like he was a sponsored athlete of consumption. He sinned frequently through overindulgence. What do we do with this information?” How to Graciously Say No. You have probably run into the same conclusion I have: it’s often far harder to say no than to say yes. Yet sometimes the best thing we can do for ourselves and for others is to say no. Reagan Rose offers some thoughts on that (for those who have the ability to say no, of course, since in many occupations saying no isn’t an option). Can’t You Let Me Just Attend? “We live in a chronically noncommittal society. No wonder, then, that this has been dubbed the age of anxiety. Surely, covenant relationships aren’t the only cure, but it might surprise us how much stability of soul comes from deep, reliable, unflighty fellowship.” David Mathis explains why commitment to a particular church is so important, even if it’s extremely countercultural. When Morningness Is Confused With Godliness. I always enjoy an article that provides an alternate view of something we almost take for granted. That’s the case with this one from Cole Mizel, who is concerned that we may be too quick to imply that the best Christians do their daily devotions in the morning. “But is it really more virtuous to be a morning person than a night owl?” Everyone Doesn’t React the Same Way. J.V. Fesko makes a simple but wise point here: everyone doesn’t respond to preaching the same way. Because that’s the case, we should not assume that those who display a lot of emotion are necessarily being impacted more than those who do not. “Tears are not the sacrament of a changed heart, the visible sign and seal of an anguished and healed soul.” Evangelising With a Dog. I enjoyed reading Peter Barnes’s account of evangelizing with a dog. “My doctor, who is half my size, suggested as tactfully as he could, what was obvious to all – that my squash days were over, and that I should take to walking the dog in the morning and at lunchtime. So this has become my nod to the need for daily exercise. Being one of the few beings on the planet who still buys a hard copy of a daily newspaper, I take my hound – a cocker spaniel with unusually short legs – with me of a morning. On the way, on most days at least, we pass a stand where two, sometimes three, Jehovah’s Witness ladies seek to engage people in conversation.” New Book Releases New Christian books tend to release on Tuesdays. Here are just a few of the books that have become available today. For a more thorough list—and there are several more than I have listed here—visit This Week in Christian Books . From Silence to Sinai: A Guided Tour of Exodus , by Alex Duke. “Alex Duke walks readers through the book of Exodus to show that nothing in God’s story is wasted–not the silence, not the ten signs, and ",
   "content_zh": "让我来提醒你一些你可能不知道的网站的特征。 我有一页越来越多的Liturgies 旨在帮助当地教会的崇拜。 我还提供书中许多类别的建议,以及圣经每本书的最佳评论者名单。\n\n如果你喜欢我每天分享的引文图形, 你可以在SquareQuotes找到它们。 教堂可以免费下载。 今天的销售和交易协议包括几本旨在帮助有共同关切的妇女的“心谈”迷你书:感觉像父母的失败、领导差的丈夫、怀疑等等。\n\n也能找到Greg Gifford的《谎言》, 建议阅读啤酒,牛肉和玩家的海军剪彩. 这是一篇关于C. S. 刘易斯和他一生的恶习的精彩文章—— 每天三包的吸烟习惯, 饮食习惯与奢侈相邻,\n\n“如果你是新人, 并且你从孩子的书里知道刘易斯, 让我们指出一个显而易见的问题: 我们普遍尊崇的20世纪伟大的圣人之一, 他经常通过过度的放纵而犯罪。 我们如何处理这些信息?” 如何是圣洁地说不.\n\n你可能也得出了同样的结论:拒绝往往比答应困难得多。 然而,有时候,我们能够为自己和他人做的最好的事情是拒绝。 里根 里根 里根 当然,因为在许多职业中, 不能让我出席吗?\n\n“我们生活在一个长期不作出承诺的社会。 难怪这被称为焦虑时代。 当然,契约关系并非唯一的治疗方法,但可能令我们惊讶的是,灵魂的稳定性来自深厚、可靠、不飞翔的友谊。 大卫·马西斯(David Mathis)解释为什么对特定教会的承诺如此重要,\n\n当早晨与真主争论的时候, 我总是喜欢一篇文章 提供替代的观点 我们几乎认为理所当然。 这名来自科尔·米泽尔(Cole Mizel), 他担心我们可能太快了,\n\n但做一个早晨的人,比做夜间的猫头鹰要好得多吗? 每个人都不照旧。 J. V. Fesko在这里提出一个简单但明智的观点:每个人都不会以同样的方式对布道作出回应。 因为情况就是这样,我们不应该假设那些表现出许多情感的人会比那些没有情绪的人受到更大的影响。\n\n“恐怖不是改变心灵的圣洁,也不是痛苦和治愈的灵魂的明显标志和封印。” 以狗为先导. 我很喜欢读彼得·巴恩斯关于用狗传福音的说法。\n\n“我的医生,有我一半的体型,他尽可能巧妙地暗示,对所有人都显而易见的是,我的壁球日已过去,我应该在早上和午餐时间走狗。 因此,这已经成为我需要每天锻炼的点头。\n\n作为地球上少数仍然购买日报硬拷贝的人之一,我每天早上都会带着我的猎犬——一只腿特别短的鸡鸡。 在路上,至少几天,我们通过一个立场,让两个、有时是三个耶和华见证人的女士寻求与人们对话。 新书发行新基督教书籍往往在星期二发行.\n\n以下仅列出今日已有的几本书. 更详尽的列表(还有我在此列出的), 从寂静到西奈:出海之旅的导游,由Alex Duke主演. \" 亚历克斯·杜克通过\"出埃及记\"让读者看到,上帝的故事中没有任何东西是浪费的,而不是沉默,而不是十种迹象,",
